rence to, or justified deviation from, professional standards in routine and emergency anesthesia practice."], "evaluation_items": ["The facilitator was effective in presenting the material.", "Teaching methods were effective.", "Online learning format facilitated learning.", "The content was related to the objectives.", "My personal learning objectives were met."], "rating_scale": {"1": "Excellent", "2": "Good", "3": "Adequate", "4": "Fair", "5": "Poor"}, "rating_scale_description": "Rate each item: 1 Excellent, 2 Good, 3 Adequate, 4 Fair, 5 Poor. All ratings and written responses are required.", "question_count": 15, "bank_size": 25, "passing_score": 12, "max_attempts": 3, "resource_prefix": "course3_module4", "page_count": 27, "document_sha256": "b65305d2c3aad20813316abdf691052b2ea1383c1ac0dc6e0d5eedd6af9a0e7f", "content_status": "provider_authorized_final"}, {"id": "course_3_module_5", "number": 5, "title": "Medication Errors & the Anesthesia Provider", "short_title": "Medication Errors", "credits": 2.0, "pharmacology_credits": 0.0, "pain_credits": 0.0, "objectives": ["Differentiate active errors, latent errors, slips, mistakes, near misses, adverse drug events, and high-alert medication events as they apply to nurse anesthesia practice.", "Identify high-risk CRNA medication scenarios across induction, maintenance, emergence, regional and neuraxial anesthesia, obstetrics, pediatrics, procedural sedation, and nonoperating-room anesthesia (NORA).", "Analyze the root causes of anesthesia medication errors using a systems-based framework that addresses human factors, medication factors, workflow, technology, environment, and organizational culture.", "Apply the 2024 Anesthesia Patient Safety Foundation (APSF) Stoelting Consensus Recommendations and the APSF STPC framework (Standardization, Technology, Pharmacy/prefilled syringes, Culture) to perioperative medication-safety decisions.", "Recognize look-alike/sound-alike (LASA) drug pairs that most commonly cause wron