tetric anesthesia carries disproportionate professional liability exposure for CRNAs and interpret current U.S. maternal mortality trends (CDC 2022\u20132024 data) and the role of anesthesia care in preventable maternal death and severe maternal morbidity.", "Apply ASHRM-informed, SOAP-endorsed, and ACOG-endorsed risk-management principles \u2014 including standardization, closed-loop communication, contemporaneous documentation, event reporting, disclosure, multidisciplinary simulation, and coordinated claims readiness \u2014 to everyday CRNA obstetric anesthesia practice.", "Identify obstetric airway liability risks and describe evidence-based CRNA strategies for difficult-airway preparation, aspiration prevention, failed-intubation response, and safe conversion from neuraxial to general anesthesia during emergency cesarean delivery.", "Recognize hemorrhage-related liability risk and outline preparation for massive transfusion, maternal resuscitation, blood-product planning, and escalation using ACOG Practice Bulletin 183 / AIM Safety Bundle principles during postpartum hemorrhage and placenta accreta spectrum.", "Evaluate neuraxial anesthesia complications \u2014 including failed or inadequate block, high or total spinal, local anesthetic systemic toxicity (LAST), post-dural puncture headache, epidural hematoma, infection, medication error, and postpartum neurologic deficit \u2014 using the 2023 ASA Statement on Neurologic Complications of Neuraxial Analgesia/Anesthesia in Obstetrics and the 2021 SOAP Thrombocytopenia Consensus.", "Describe amniotic fluid embolism (AFE) recognition, differential diagnosis, and coordinated maternal cardiac-arrest response consistent with the SOAP/AHA (2014, updated 2023) consensus on cardiac arrest in pregnancy, including 4-minute perimortem cesarean decision-making.", "Analyze anesthesia liability arising from emergency cesarean delivery \u2014 including decision-to-incision time, urgency classification, failed epidural conversio