allenges to expert qualifications.", "Analyze anesthesia records, timelines, causation, and system factors using a disciplined case-analysis framework consistent with FRE 702 reliability requirements and current AANA professional standards."], "evaluation_items": ["The facilitator was effective in presenting the material.", "Teaching methods were effective.", "Online learning format facilitated learning.", "The content was related to the objectives.", "My personal learning objectives were met."], "rating_scale": {"1": "Excellent", "2": "Good", "3": "Adequate", "4": "Fair", "5": "Poor"}, "rating_scale_description": "Rate each item: 1 Excellent, 2 Good, 3 Adequate, 4 Fair, 5 Poor. All ratings and written responses are required.", "question_count": 15, "bank_size": 25, "passing_score": 12, "max_attempts": 3, "resource_prefix": "course3_module10", "page_count": 23, "document_sha256": "110ca0bffe92c7e613f0f3b769c33685fdd5c79b34c14bb117ab46101161aee1", "content_status": "provider_authorized_final"}, {"id": "course_3_module_11", "number": 11, "title": "Sentinel Events & Just-Culture Response", "short_title": "Sentinel Events and Root Cause Analysis", "credits": 1.5, "pharmacology_credits": 0.0, "pain_credits": 0.0, "objectives": ["Define sentinel event, adverse event, no-harm event, and close call/near miss using the 2024 Joint Commission Sentinel Event Policy, and describe which events are reviewable under that policy.", "Apply the RCA2 (Root Cause Analysis and Action) framework from the National Patient Safety Foundation to plan a comprehensive systematic analysis after an anesthesia-related sentinel event, including the 55-business-day timeline and action-plan components required by The Joint Commission.", "Distinguish among human error, at-risk behavior, and reckless conduct using the Marx just-culture algorithm, and describe the three duties (produce an outcome, follow a procedural rule, avoid unjustifiable risk) that the model uses to guide event review and provider response.", "Recognize the second-victim phenomenon and describe evidence-based peer support program structures (e.g., forYOU/MISE) that support CRNAs and other clinicians involved in a sentinel event.", "Describe the CANDOR (Communication and Optimal Resolution) framework, state apology and disclosure statutes, and federal and state peer-review protections that apply after an anesthesia-related adverse event.", "Identify common anesthesia-specific sentinel events, including wrong-site regional block, retained foreign body, medication error involving high-alert drugs, and unrecognized esophageal intubation, and describe the prevention strategies supported by current AANA, ASA, and APSF standards.", "Explain the CRNA's regulatory and quality-improvement reporting obligations after a sentinel event, including FDA MedWatch (21 CFR \u00a7803), manufacturer medical device reporting, state adverse-event reporting statutes, and internal event-reporting systems."], "evaluation_items": ["The facilitator was effective in presenting the material.", "Teaching methods were effective.", "Online learning format facilitated learning.", "The content was related to the objectives.", "My personal learning objectives were met."], "rating_scale": {"1": "Excellent", "2": "Good", "3": "Adequate", "4": "Fair", "5": "Poor"}, "rating_scale_description": "Rate each item: 1 Excellent, 2 Good, 3 Adequate, 4 Fair, 5 Poor. All ratings and written responses are required.", "question_count": 15, "bank_size": 25, "passing_score": 12, "max_attempts": 3, "resource_prefix": "course3_module11", "page_count": 30, "document_sha256": "d311494df576817616b8769abc819b429887a25847afa24af6b159cf20d3ebf5", "content_status": "provider_authorized_final"}], "course_id": "1047243", "course_number": 3, "has_designated_credits": false, "total_credits": 20, "pharmacology_credits": 0, "pain_credits": 0, "required_modules": 11, "purchases_enabled": false, "released": false}''');
final Map<String,dynamic> demoCourse3Banks = jsonDecode(r'''{"course_3_module_1": [{"module_id": "course_3_module_1", "question_id": "course_3_module_1_q01", "question_number": 1, "stem": "Which of the following most accurately describes how a CRNA's legal scope of practice is determined at any given moment?", "choice_a": "By the combined effect of federal law, state law, board of nursing rules, facility bylaws, payer requirements, credentialing, and professional standards \u2014 with the more restrictive applicable rule controlling", "choice_b": "By state law alone", "choice_c": "By federal Medicare Conditions of Participation alone", "choice_d": "By AANA policy alone", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "CRNA scope is determined by layered sources. When state and federal requirements overlap, the more restrictive applicable rule controls. Facility bylaws and payer contracts may be more restrictive than either.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_1", "question_id": "course_3_module_1_q02", "question_number": 2, "stem": "Per the 2026 AANA Scope of Nurse Anesthesia Practice, CRNAs/nurse anesthesiologists are formally described as:", "choice_a": "Certified nursing assistants with advanced training", "choice_b": "Advanced practice registered nurses (APRNs) licensed as independent practitioners who plan and deliver anesthesia, pain management, and related care", "choice_c": "Registered nurses functioning under physician delegation", "choice_d": "Physician assistants specializing in anesthesia", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "The 2026 AANA scope defines CRNAs/nurse anesthesiologists as APRNs licensed as independent practitioners who plan and deliver anesthesia, pain management, and related care across the lifespan.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_1", "question_id": "course_3_module_1_q03", "question_number": 3, "stem": "As of July 2026, approximately how many states plus U.S. territories have opted out of the CMS physician-supervision requirement for CRNAs?", "choice_a": "All 50 states plus Guam and Puerto Rico", "choice_b": "22 states plus Guam", "choice_c": "27 states plus Guam", "choice_d": "17 states, no territories", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Per the AANA July 2026 Fact Sheet Concerning State Opt-Outs, 27 states plus Guam have opted out. Vermont and Ohio were the most recent (both July 2026).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_1", "question_id": "course_3_module_1_q04", "question_number": 4, "stem": "A CRNA practices in a state that has opted out of the federal CMS physician-supervision requirement, but the hospital's medical staff bylaws still require anesthesiologist supervision for all cases. What must the CRNA do?", "choice_a": "Report the facility to CMS for noncompliance", "choice_b": "Refuse to work until the bylaws are amended", "choice_c": "Ignore the bylaws because the state opt-out overrides them", "choice_d": "Follow the facility bylaws because they are more restrictive; opt-out only removes the federal supervision condition and does not override facility rules", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Per AANA, an opt-out does not permit a CRNA to practice outside state law or override facility rules. Individual facilities may still require CRNAs to be physician-supervised regardless of opt-out status.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_1", "question_id": "course_3_module_1_q05", "question_number": 5, "stem": "Which federal regulation establishes the physician-supervision requirement for anesthesia services in Medicare-participating hospitals?", "choice_a": "42 CFR \u00a7482.52", "choice_b": "42 USC \u00a71395dd (EMTALA)", "choice_c": "45 CFR \u00a7164.502", "choice_d": "21 CFR \u00a71301.11", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "42 CFR \u00a7482.52 is the Anesthesia Services Condition of Participation for hospitals. \u00a7485.639 covers critical access hospitals; \u00a7416.42 covers ambulatory surgical centers.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_1", "question_id": "course_3_module_1_q06", "question_number": 6, "stem": "Per NCSBN, which of the following best describes the Independent category for CRNA practice authority?", "choice_a": "The CRNA is exempt from facility credentialing", "choice_b": "No requirement for a written collaborative agreement, no supervision, no conditions for practice (may follow a statutorily required transition period)", "choice_c": "State law requires no license or registration for CRNA practice", "choice_d": "The CRNA may practice without any state license as long as national certification is current", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Per the NCSBN CRNA Independent Practice Map, Independent means no requirement for a written collaborative agreement, no supervision, and no conditions for practice, though a statutorily required transition period may still precede independent authority.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_1", "question_id": "course_3_module_1_q07", "question_number": 7, "stem": "How does prescriptive authority differ from anesthesia practice authority for a CRNA?", "choice_a": "Prescriptive authority applies only to over-the-counter medications", "choice_b": "Anesthesia practice authority automatically confers full state and federal prescriptive authority", "choice_c": "Prescriptive authority describes prescribing pharmacologic and non-pharmacologic therapies beyond the perioperative and periprocedural periods and is legally distinct from perioperative medication administration", "choice_d": "They are the same thing under federal law", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Per NCSBN and federal regulations, prescriptive authority extends beyond the perioperative period. CRNAs may order and administer controlled substances perioperatively within anesthesia scope without independent prescriptive authority, but broader prescribing requires state prescriptive authority.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_1", "question_id": "course_3_module_1_q08", "question_number": 8, "stem": "Under what federal regulation does a CRNA obtain individual DEA registration as a mid-level practitioner?", "choice_a": "42 CFR \u00a7416.42", "choice_b": "42 CFR \u00a7482.52", "choice_c": "45 CFR \u00a7160.103", "choice_d": "21 CFR \u00a71301.11 (with mid-level classification under \u00a71301.13)", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "21 CFR \u00a71301.11 establishes the general DEA registration requirement; \u00a71301.13 addresses mid-level practitioner classification, which is the applicable pathway for CRNA DEA registration.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_1", "question_id": "course_3_module_1_q09", "question_number": 9, "stem": "A CRNA is asked to add ketamine infusion services in an office-based setting. Which of the following is the most complete pre-service verification?", "choice_a": "Confirm state law authorization, professional standard support, facility privileging, individual documented competence, and controlled-substance / DEA compliance", "choice_b": "Check state prescriptive authority only", "choice_c": "Rely on the CMS opt-out because ketamine is administered by a practitioner", "choice_d": "Check facility privileges only", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "The five-step framework requires verification across state authorization, professional standard, competence, facility privileging, and downstream documentation/CSR requirements. CMS opt-out is irrelevant to office-based ketamine services.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_1", "question_id": "course_3_module_1_q10", "question_number": 10, "stem": "Which statement about the Nurse Licensure Compact (NLC) is correct for a CRNA planning multistate travel practice?", "choice_a": "The NLC has been repealed and no compact exists", "choice_b": "The NLC covers RN and LPN licensure only; APRN licensure is not covered by the NLC \u2014 a separate APRN Compact is being implemented state by state", "choice_c": "The NLC allows practice in any state as long as national certification is current", "choice_d": "The NLC covers RN, LPN, and all APRN licensure across member states", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "The NLC does not cover APRN licensure. The APRN Compact adopted by NCSBN in 2020 is being implemented state by state; CRNAs must verify current APRN licensure status in every state where they practice.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_1", "question_id": "course_3_module_1_q11", "question_number": 11, "stem": "A state opt-out under CMS \u00a7482.52(c) is effective:", "choice_a": "Upon publication in the Federal Register", "choice_b": "Only after unanimous state legislature approval", "choice_c": "Upon submission by the governor's letter of attestation to CMS", "choice_d": "60 days after CMS approval", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "The 2001 CMS rule permits a state governor to opt out via letter of attestation; the opt-out is effective upon submission. Withdrawal is likewise effective upon submission.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_1", "question_id": "course_3_module_1_q12", "question_number": 12, "stem": "Which of the following is required in a governor's opt-out letter of attestation to CMS?", "choice_a": "A federal impact statement filed with HHS", "choice_b": "Written concurrence from the state anesthesiology society", "choice_c": "Certification that the state legislature has voted in favor", "choice_d": "Consultation with state boards of medicine and nursing about access and quality; statement that opt-out is in the best interests of state citizens; consistency with state law", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Per the 2001 CMS rule, the governor's attestation must affirm consultation with state boards of medicine and nursing, that opt-out is in the best interests of citizens, and that it is consistent with state law.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_1", "question_id": "course_3_module_1_q13", "question_number": 13, "stem": "When state law authorizes independent CRNA practice but a Medicare-participating facility's medical staff bylaws require physician supervision, the CRNA's anesthesia record should:", "choice_a": "Accurately reflect whether required supervisory contact actually occurred; documentation must never imply supervision that did not happen", "choice_b": "Copy prior anesthetic records for consistency", "choice_c": "Omit any reference to supervision to avoid billing complications", "choice_d": "Document supervision in the record regardless of whether it occurred", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Documentation is a legal record. It must accurately reflect the applicable practice model; misrepresentation may create billing, regulatory, malpractice, or disciplinary risk.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_1", "question_id": "course_3_module_1_q14", "question_number": 14, "stem": "Which resource is most authoritative for verifying the current opt-out status of a specific state?", "choice_a": "A commercial locum tenens agency", "choice_b": "The AANA Fact Sheet Concerning State Opt-Outs (updated periodically) and the CMS Anesthesiologists Information Center", "choice_c": "The state department of transportation", "choice_d": "A general internet search result", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Primary sources are authoritative: the AANA Fact Sheet is maintained specifically for opt-out tracking, and the CMS Anesthesiologists Information Center publishes federal guidance.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_1", "question_id": "course_3_module_1_q15", "question_number": 15, "stem": "A CRNA moves from an opt-out state with full independent practice authority to a state that requires a written collaborative agreement with a physician. What is the correct next step before beginning practice?", "choice_a": "Rely on the prior state's independent authority until license renewal", "choice_b": "Assume federal CMS opt-out from the prior state carries over", "choice_c": "Verify state APRN license, obtain the required written collaborative agreement, update DEA registration address, verify state CSR, review facility bylaws, and confirm malpractice coverage in the new state", "choice_d": "Practice only under general supervision without a written agreement", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Multistate practice requires per-state verification: licensure, agreements, DEA/CSR, facility credentialing, and malpractice must all be current in the new state.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_1", "question_id": "course_3_module_1_q16", "question_number": 16, "stem": "Which of the following is NOT a legally required verification when a CRNA begins prescribing controlled substances outside the perioperative period in a new state?", "choice_a": "State controlled-substance registration (if required by the state)", "choice_b": "Current DEA registration under 21 CFR \u00a71301.11", "choice_c": "State APRN prescriptive authority", "choice_d": "Local hospital's marketing department approval", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Hospital marketing approval is not a legal requirement. DEA registration, state CSR, and state APRN prescriptive authority are all required prior to non-perioperative controlled-substance prescribing.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_1", "question_id": "course_3_module_1_q17", "question_number": 17, "stem": "Which statement about \u201cmedical direction\u201d is most accurate?", "choice_a": "It is primarily a Medicare billing and anesthesia care-team concept rather than a state licensure requirement, and terminology varies across states", "choice_b": "It has been eliminated from federal regulation", "choice_c": "It is identical to \u201cindependent practice\u201d", "choice_d": "It is a state licensure requirement in all 50 states", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Medical direction is largely a Medicare billing concept (TEFRA / 7 steps) and anesthesia care-team term. State licensure terminology (supervision, collaboration, consultation) is distinct and jurisdiction-specific.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_1", "question_id": "course_3_module_1_q18", "question_number": 18, "stem": "A CRNA in a physician-supervision state accepts a locum tenens assignment in a CMS opt-out state at a critical access hospital. What determines whether the CRNA must be supervised at that facility?", "choice_a": "Only federal CMS rules, because CAHs are federally regulated", "choice_b": "The combination of the destination state's practice authority law and the CAH's own bylaws and medical staff rules \u2014 federal supervision has been removed by the opt-out, so state and facility rules govern", "choice_c": "The rules of the state where the CRNA obtained anesthesia education", "choice_d": "The rules of the CRNA's home state", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "The opt-out removes the federal supervision Condition of Participation, so the destination state's practice authority law and the facility's bylaws control whether supervision is required at that CAH.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_1", "question_id": "course_3_module_1_q19", "question_number": 19, "stem": "Per the 2026 AANA scope, the scope of an individual CRNA's practice is determined by:", "choice_a": "The CRNA's malpractice insurer", "choice_b": "The physician's preference", "choice_c": "Education, experience, local law, state law, federal law, and organization policy", "choice_d": "AANA policy alone", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "The 2026 AANA scope explicitly states that the individual CRNA's scope is determined by education, experience, local law, state law, federal law, and organization policy.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_1", "question_id": "course_3_module_1_q20", "question_number": 20, "stem": "What is the practical difference between legal scope and individual competence for a CRNA?", "choice_a": "They are identical", "choice_b": "Competence overrides legal scope in all cases", "choice_c": "Facility privileging overrides both", "choice_d": "Legal scope defines what a CRNA may do; individual competence defines what a CRNA should do safely and ethically; facility privileging defines what a CRNA is authorized to do at a specific site \u2014 all three must align", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Safe practice requires alignment among legal authority, individual competence, and institutional authorization. A procedure may be legal but require additional competence or facility privileges before performance.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_1", "question_id": "course_3_module_1_q21", "question_number": 21, "stem": "Which of the following is the correct sequence for CRNA legal research when preparing to add a new office-based anesthesia service?", "choice_a": "State board of nursing \u2192 related state statutes (pharmacy, CSR, office-based rules) \u2192 federal (CMS, DEA) \u2192 professional standards \u2192 facility policy", "choice_b": "Facility policy \u2192 payer contract only", "choice_c": "DEA only, because controlled substances are involved", "choice_d": "Search social media first, then confirm with the facility", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "The recommended research order is board of nursing \u2192 other state statutes \u2192 federal \u2192 professional standards \u2192 facility, then reconcile with the more restrictive rule governing.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_1", "question_id": "course_3_module_1_q22", "question_number": 22, "stem": "If a CRNA in a supervised practice state is asked by a surgeon to perform a procedure that exceeds the CRNA's granted facility privileges, the CRNA should:", "choice_a": "Perform the procedure to maintain the OR schedule", "choice_b": "Pause the activity, seek clarification, escalate through the chain of command, and document the consultation before proceeding \u2014 even under supervision, the CRNA remains accountable for practicing within licensure, competence, standards, and facility policy", "choice_c": "Refuse and report the surgeon to the state medical board", "choice_d": "Ask the surgeon to co-sign the record and proceed", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Even under supervision or medical direction, the CRNA retains personal accountability. Requests exceeding scope, privileges, or competence should be paused, escalated, and documented before proceeding.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_1", "question_id": "course_3_module_1_q23", "question_number": 23, "stem": "A payer contract requires physician co-signature on every CRNA anesthesia record, even in an opt-out state with full state independent practice authority. Which of the following is the most accurate compliance conclusion?", "choice_a": "State opt-out overrides all payer contracts", "choice_b": "The CRNA may ignore the payer requirement because state law is independent", "choice_c": "Payer contracts may be more restrictive than state or federal law and must be followed as long as the contract is in force; the CRNA should confirm the requirement with billing and risk management", "choice_d": "Payer contracts have no legal effect on CRNA practice", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Payer contracts can create requirements more restrictive than state or federal law. The CRNA must comply while the contract is in force and coordinate with billing and risk management if the requirement is unclear.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_1", "question_id": "course_3_module_1_q24", "question_number": 24, "stem": "Which of the following is a legitimate use of the AANA Practice in Your State resource?", "choice_a": "As a replacement for state board of nursing advisory opinions", "choice_b": "As an authoritative source for facility bylaws", "choice_c": "As a substitute for reading the state nurse practice act", "choice_d": "As a starting point that identifies opt-out status, prescriptive authority map, and quicklinks, to be verified against the actual state nurse practice act, board rules, and current statute", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Professional resources like AANA Practice in Your State are excellent starting points but should not replace review of the current state nurse practice act, board rules, and advisory opinions.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_1", "question_id": "course_3_module_1_q25", "question_number": 25, "stem": "When a state has full independent CRNA practice authority but the facility bylaws and payer both require anesthesiologist supervision, the applicable operational rule is:", "choice_a": "The most restrictive applicable rule \u2014 in this case, the facility bylaws and payer requirement \u2014 must be followed while in that facility and under that payer", "choice_b": "Only the CRNA's individual judgment", "choice_c": "State law, because it is the most permissive source", "choice_d": "Federal Medicare rules, regardless of opt-out", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "The bedrock rule of layered CRNA scope is that the more restrictive applicable requirement always controls. State independent authority does not override facility bylaws or payer contracts.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}], "course_3_module_2": [{"module_id": "course_3_module_2", "question_id": "course_3_module_2_q01", "question_number": 1, "stem": "According to AANA Standard 4 and the April 2024 CMS revised interpretive guidance, informed consent for anesthesia is best described as:", "choice_a": "A communication process that includes disclosure, understanding, voluntariness, capacity, and authorization", "choice_b": "A signature witnessed by a circulating nurse before induction", "choice_c": "A verbal agreement that the anesthesia team may proceed", "choice_d": "A signed consent form obtained by the preoperative nurse", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Both AANA Standard 4 and the April 2024 CMS interpretive guidance define informed consent as a process, not the signing of a form. The five core elements are disclosure, understanding, voluntariness, decision-making capacity, and authorization.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_2", "question_id": "course_3_module_2_q02", "question_number": 2, "stem": "A patient signs the surgical consent form. On arrival to the OR, the patient states, \"No one told me about the anesthesia.\" The CRNA's most appropriate next action is to:", "choice_a": "Have the circulating nurse re-witness the signature", "choice_b": "Stop and complete the anesthesia consent discussion before proceeding", "choice_c": "Administer midazolam and complete the discussion in the OR", "choice_d": "Proceed with induction because the surgical consent is signed", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "A signed surgical consent does not authorize the anesthesia plan. The CRNA should stop and complete the anesthesia consent discussion. Consent must not be obtained after sedating medication has impaired capacity.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_2", "question_id": "course_3_module_2_q03", "question_number": 3, "stem": "Under the April 2024 CMS revised hospital interpretive guidance, informed consent must disclose all of the following EXCEPT:", "choice_a": "The identity of the practitioner who will perform the procedure", "choice_b": "The participation of medical students, residents, or fellows", "choice_c": "The specific billing code assigned to the procedure", "choice_d": "Consent for a medical student to perform a sensitive examination under anesthesia", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "The April 2024 guidance requires disclosure of who will perform the procedure, trainee participation, and explicit consent for sensitive examinations. Billing codes are not part of the consent-disclosure requirement.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_2", "question_id": "course_3_module_2_q04", "question_number": 4, "stem": "Which AANA Standard specifically addresses informed consent for anesthesia care?", "choice_a": "Standard 2 \u2014 Preanesthesia Patient Assessment and Evaluation", "choice_b": "Standard 3 \u2014 Plan for Anesthesia Care", "choice_c": "Standard 1 \u2014 Patient's Rights", "choice_d": "Standard 4 \u2014 Informed Consent for Anesthesia Care and Related Services", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Standard 4, titled \"Informed Consent for Anesthesia Care and Related Services,\" requires the CRNA to obtain and document, or verify the documentation, that consent has been given for the planned anesthesia care.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_2", "question_id": "course_3_module_2_q05", "question_number": 5, "stem": "Which regulation requires a properly executed informed consent form to be in the patient's medical record before surgery, except in emergencies?", "choice_a": "42 CFR \u00a7482.51(b)(2)", "choice_b": "42 CFR \u00a7482.24(c)(4)(v)", "choice_c": "42 CFR \u00a7482.13(b)(2)", "choice_d": "42 CFR \u00a7482.52", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "42 CFR \u00a7482.51(b)(2) specifically addresses the requirement for a properly executed informed-consent form for the operation in the medical record before surgery, except in emergencies. \u00a7482.52 addresses anesthesia service supervision, not consent forms.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_2", "question_id": "course_3_module_2_q06", "question_number": 6, "stem": "A circulating nurse witnesses a patient's signature on the anesthesia consent form. This action:", "choice_a": "Establishes that informed consent was obtained", "choice_b": "Confirms that the correct person signed the form, but does not establish that informed consent was obtained", "choice_c": "Substitutes for the CRNA's obligation to verify understanding", "choice_d": "Fulfills AANA Standard 4 on behalf of the CRNA", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Witnessing a signature confirms only the identity of the signer. It does not establish that a valid consent discussion occurred. Standard 4 places the consent responsibility on the anesthesia professional.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_2", "question_id": "course_3_module_2_q07", "question_number": 7, "stem": "A CRNA cares for a Spanish-speaking patient with limited English proficiency. The most appropriate approach to consent is to:", "choice_a": "Use the patient's adult family member to interpret", "choice_b": "Use written English materials and hand gestures", "choice_c": "Use a qualified medical interpreter and document the interpreter's identity, language, and service ID", "choice_d": "Proceed and document \"consent obtained\" once the patient signs", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Qualified medical interpreters are the standard for consent with LEP patients. Family members should not be used as primary interpreters whenever a qualified medical interpreter is available. Interpreter identity and service ID should be documented.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_2", "question_id": "course_3_module_2_q08", "question_number": 8, "stem": "A patient with capacity refuses the only anesthetic technique the CRNA believes is safe. The CRNA should:", "choice_a": "Administer sedation without consent so the case can proceed", "choice_b": "Document \"patient refused\" and cancel the case without further discussion", "choice_c": "Proceed with an anesthetic technique the CRNA believes is unsafe because the patient has autonomy", "choice_d": "Reassess capacity, clarify the refusal, explain risks of refusal, discuss alternatives, and escalate \u2014 and not proceed with an unsafe plan", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Informed refusal requires a structured response: reassess capacity, clarify the refusal, explain risks of refusal, discuss alternatives, involve appropriate team members, do not proceed with an unsafe plan, and document. Refusal is not a consent problem to \"work around.\"", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_2", "question_id": "course_3_module_2_q09", "question_number": 9, "stem": "Which of the following documentation entries best satisfies AANA Standard 4 for a general anesthetic?", "choice_a": "\"Discussed general anesthesia with endotracheal intubation, including sore throat, dental injury, aspiration, PONV, awareness, cardiopulmonary complications, medication reaction, and rare risk of serious injury or death. Alternatives and questions reviewed. Patient verbalized understanding and agreed.\"", "choice_b": "\"Consent form signed pre-op.\"", "choice_c": "\"See surgical consent.\"", "choice_d": "\"Risks explained. Patient consents.\"", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Specific, individualized language identifying the technique and representative material risks satisfies Standard 4 and the CMS 2024 guidance. Vague entries such as \"risks explained\" or references to a surgical consent do not.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_2", "question_id": "course_3_module_2_q10", "question_number": 10, "stem": "An intraoperative crisis converts a peripheral nerve block with sedation into a general anesthetic. Regarding consent, the CRNA should:", "choice_a": "Rely on the surgical consent alone", "choice_b": "Manage the airway and hemodynamics, then document the clinical rationale for the conversion afterward", "choice_c": "Stop the case to obtain new signed consent", "choice_d": "Have the surgeon obtain new consent while the CRNA manages the airway", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "For a material plan change during an emergency, care priorities take precedence. The CRNA should manage the crisis, then document the clinical rationale for the conversion in the anesthesia record.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_2", "question_id": "course_3_module_2_q11", "question_number": 11, "stem": "AANA Standard 2 relates to informed consent because it requires:", "choice_a": "Disclosure of anesthesia billing codes to the patient", "choice_b": "A signed consent form before surgery", "choice_c": "A preanesthesia evaluation and anesthesia-focused physical assessment that informs individualized risk disclosure", "choice_d": "The CRNA to obtain consent from the surgeon", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Standard 2 requires the CRNA to perform or verify a preanesthesia evaluation and anesthesia-focused physical assessment. This assessment identifies patient-specific risks that must be individualized in the consent discussion required by Standard 4.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_2", "question_id": "course_3_module_2_q12", "question_number": 12, "stem": "A trauma patient arrives obtunded, hemorrhaging, and unable to consent. No surrogate is reachable. The CRNA proceeds under the emergency exception. The strongest legal protection comes from:", "choice_a": "Having the circulating nurse sign the consent form", "choice_b": "The verbal report from the EMS provider", "choice_c": "Waiting until after the case to notify risk management", "choice_d": "Documenting the emergency condition, efforts to contact a surrogate, inability to obtain consent, and the clinical reason anesthesia could not be delayed", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Emergency-exception protection is strongest when the record documents the emergency, surrogate-contact efforts, inability to obtain consent, and clinical rationale for immediate anesthesia care.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_2", "question_id": "course_3_module_2_q13", "question_number": 13, "stem": "A 16-year-old presents for cesarean delivery. Regarding consent, the CRNA should:", "choice_a": "Follow facility policy and applicable state law regarding pregnant-minor consent and document who provided consent, their authority, capacity, and interpreter use if applicable", "choice_b": "Assume the parents must consent regardless of state law", "choice_c": "Delay the case until legal consultation is obtained", "choice_d": "Assume the minor may consent regardless of state law", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Pregnant-minor consent rules vary by state. The CRNA should document that facility policy and applicable state law were followed, and identify the person providing consent and their authority.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_2", "question_id": "course_3_module_2_q14", "question_number": 14, "stem": "Under the April 2024 CMS interpretive guidance, consent for a medical student to perform a sensitive examination under anesthesia:", "choice_a": "Is automatically included in consent to the underlying procedure", "choice_b": "Must be obtained explicitly, separate from consent to the underlying procedure", "choice_c": "Is required only if the student is unlicensed", "choice_d": "Is not required if the student is supervised", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "The April 2024 CMS guidance explicitly requires informed consent before a medical student performs a sensitive examination, including examinations under anesthesia. Consent to the underlying procedure does not automatically authorize the student's involvement.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_2", "question_id": "course_3_module_2_q15", "question_number": 15, "stem": "AANA Standard 3 (Plan for Anesthesia Care) directs the CRNA to develop and document:", "choice_a": "A plan approved by the surgeon before consent is obtained", "choice_b": "A plan that does not include reasonable alternatives", "choice_c": "A patient-specific plan based on the preanesthesia assessment, procedural requirements, and available resources, in collaboration with the patient", "choice_d": "A generic anesthesia plan applicable to all patients", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Standard 3 requires a patient-specific plan developed in collaboration with the patient (and legal representative when indicated). Reasonable alternatives are part of the consent discussion required by Standard 4.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_2", "question_id": "course_3_module_2_q16", "question_number": 16, "stem": "A Jehovah's Witness patient signs the anesthesia consent but has a documented advance directive refusing blood products. The CRNA's most appropriate action is to:", "choice_a": "Ignore the advance directive because consent was signed", "choice_b": "Cancel the case", "choice_c": "Ask the family to override the advance directive", "choice_d": "Verify and document the advance directive per AANA Standard 4 and identify acceptable and unacceptable products in advance", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Standard 4 requires the CRNA to verify, review, or document applicable advance directives and consent for blood products. Acceptable and unacceptable products should be identified in advance and escalated when refusal materially changes risk.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_2", "question_id": "course_3_module_2_q17", "question_number": 17, "stem": "A patient with dementia is scheduled for cataract surgery under MAC. Regarding consent, the CRNA should:", "choice_a": "Reassess decision-specific capacity, involve the legally authorized representative when required, and document capacity assessment", "choice_b": "Proceed without consent under the emergency exception", "choice_c": "Have the surgeon sign the consent", "choice_d": "Assume the patient can consent because they can state their name", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Capacity is decision-specific and can fluctuate. The CRNA should reassess capacity, involve a legally authorized representative when required, and document the capacity assessment.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_2", "question_id": "course_3_module_2_q18", "question_number": 18, "stem": "Which of the following statements about CRNA liability and team practice is TRUE?", "choice_a": "The CRNA has no consent responsibility if the anesthesiologist obtained consent", "choice_b": "In every practice model, the CRNA remains responsible for actions within their role, competence, documentation, and communication", "choice_c": "The CRNA's liability is eliminated by a signed consent form", "choice_d": "The CRNA's liability is transferred to the anesthesiologist in a medically directed model", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "In medically directed, collaborative, independent, and anesthesia-care-team models, each clinician remains responsible for actions within their own role and documentation. Team practice does not eliminate individual CRNA liability.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_2", "question_id": "course_3_module_2_q19", "question_number": 19, "stem": "For a peripheral nerve block, the anesthesia consent discussion should specifically include which of the following risks?", "choice_a": "Post-dural-puncture headache and high spinal", "choice_b": "Malignant hyperthermia and awareness", "choice_c": "Block failure, incomplete analgesia, bleeding, infection, LAST, nerve injury, and (when anatomically relevant) pneumothorax", "choice_d": "Fetal hypoxia and uterine rupture", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Peripheral nerve block risks are technique-specific: block failure, LAST, nerve injury, and pneumothorax where anatomically relevant. Malignant hyperthermia and awareness relate to general anesthesia; PDPH and high spinal relate to neuraxial techniques.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_2", "question_id": "course_3_module_2_q20", "question_number": 20, "stem": "A CRNA is caring for a patient in an anesthesia-care-team model. The anesthesiologist obtained consent one day earlier. Before induction, the CRNA should:", "choice_a": "Proceed to induction without further discussion", "choice_b": "Re-obtain consent from the beginning", "choice_c": "Ask the surgeon to re-obtain consent", "choice_d": "Verify that the patient still agrees with the plan, that no major clinical changes have occurred, and that the identity of the practitioner performing the anesthesia has been disclosed", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "When consent was obtained earlier by another anesthesia professional, the CRNA should verify continued agreement, absence of major clinical changes, and \u2014 per April 2024 CMS guidance \u2014 disclosure of the actual practitioner performing the care.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_2", "question_id": "course_3_module_2_q21", "question_number": 21, "stem": "Which of the following BEST describes the interaction between AANA Standards 2, 3, and 4?", "choice_a": "Standards 2, 3, and 4 establish the sequence of assessment, planning, and consent that the CRNA must complete before delivering care", "choice_b": "Standards 2, 3, and 4 apply only to anesthesiologists", "choice_c": "Standard 4 replaces Standards 2 and 3 for informed consent", "choice_d": "Standards 2 and 3 are optional when Standard 4 is satisfied", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Standards 2, 3, and 4 work together: assess the patient, develop a patient-specific plan, and obtain or verify informed consent before care is delivered.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_2", "question_id": "course_3_module_2_q22", "question_number": 22, "stem": "A patient signed the anesthesia consent 20 minutes ago and has since received IV midazolam 2 mg. Before proceeding, the CRNA should:", "choice_a": "Cancel the case", "choice_b": "Recognize that sedating medication may impair current capacity and document the consent was completed prior to sedation", "choice_c": "Have the patient re-sign the consent form now", "choice_d": "Assume the previously signed consent remains valid regardless of sedation", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Consent should be obtained before sedating medication impairs capacity. The CRNA should document that consent was completed prior to sedation. Re-signing after sedation does not cure the process problem.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_2", "question_id": "course_3_module_2_q23", "question_number": 23, "stem": "For an obstetric patient with a labor epidural in place who now requires emergency cesarean delivery under general anesthesia, the CRNA should:", "choice_a": "Rely on the labor-epidural consent alone", "choice_b": "Delay the case for a formal consent discussion regardless of fetal or maternal status", "choice_c": "Recognize that labor-epidural consent does not automatically extend to emergency general anesthesia and, when time permits, obtain additional consent \u2014 otherwise document the emergency rationale afterward", "choice_d": "Ask the obstetrician to sign consent on the patient's behalf", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Labor-epidural consent does not automatically authorize emergency general anesthesia. When time permits, obtain additional consent; when it does not, document the emergency clinical rationale.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_2", "question_id": "course_3_module_2_q24", "question_number": 24, "stem": "Under 42 CFR \u00a7482.24(c)(4)(v), the anesthesia consent form must be:", "choice_a": "Notarized by a hospital attorney", "choice_b": "Signed by two witnesses", "choice_c": "Filed with the state board of nursing", "choice_d": "Properly executed and included in the medical record for procedures specified by medical staff or by federal or state law", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "42 CFR \u00a7482.24(c)(4)(v) requires properly executed informed-consent forms to be included in the medical record for procedures specified by medical staff or by federal or state law.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_2", "question_id": "course_3_module_2_q25", "question_number": 25, "stem": "A CRNA wants to reduce medico-legal exposure related to anesthesia consent. The single most effective action is to:", "choice_a": "Personally complete the anesthesia consent discussion, individualize risk disclosure, invite questions, and document the discussion \u2014 before sedating medications are given", "choice_b": "Use a preprinted risks list without individualizing", "choice_c": "Delegate consent to the preoperative nurse", "choice_d": "Rely on the surgical consent form", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Personal completion of the discussion by the CRNA delivering the care, individualized risk disclosure, and contemporaneous documentation \u2014 before sedating medications impair capacity \u2014 are the most effective risk-management actions.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}], "course_3_module_3": [{"module_id": "course_3_module_3", "question_id": "course_3_module_3_q01", "question_number": 1, "stem": "The anesthesia record is best described legally as:", "choice_a": "A contemporaneous clinical account created close in time to the care and frequently the most reliable perioperative record in litigation, board, and credentialing review", "choice_b": "A summary written after the case for quality improvement", "choice_c": "An administrative billing form only", "choice_d": "An internal safety document that is not discoverable", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Courts and reviewers treat the anesthesia record as the most reliable account of perioperative care because it is created contemporaneously. It is a clinical, billing, compliance, and \u2014 if needed \u2014 liability-defense document.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_3", "question_id": "course_3_module_3_q02", "question_number": 2, "stem": "Under CMS 42 CFR \u00a7482.52 and Appendix A, the preanesthesia evaluation must be documented:", "choice_a": "Within 7 days of the procedure by any physician", "choice_b": "Within 48 hours before surgery or the procedure by an individual qualified to administer anesthesia", "choice_c": "At any time up to 30 days before the procedure", "choice_d": "Within 24 hours before the procedure by a registered nurse", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "42 CFR \u00a7482.52 and the CMS Appendix A Interpretive Guidelines require the preanesthesia evaluation to be performed and documented within 48 hours before surgery or the procedure by a qualified individual.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_3", "question_id": "course_3_module_3_q03", "question_number": 3, "stem": "Under CMS requirements, the postanesthesia evaluation must be:", "choice_a": "Completed within 4 hours by the PACU RN", "choice_b": "Optional if the case is uncomplicated", "choice_c": "Completed no later than 48 hours after the procedure by a qualified individual, after the patient has recovered sufficiently to participate", "choice_d": "Completed during emergence in the OR", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "CMS requires the postanesthesia evaluation to be completed within 48 hours after surgery, by an individual qualified to administer anesthesia, and after the patient has recovered sufficiently to participate \u2014 not during immediate emergence.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_3", "question_id": "course_3_module_3_q04", "question_number": 4, "stem": "Which format is most useful for documenting clinical judgment during an intraoperative event?", "choice_a": "\"Patient stable\"", "choice_b": "SBAR only", "choice_c": "Free-text summary written after the case", "choice_d": "Observation\u2013Interpretation\u2013Action\u2013Response\u2013Plan (OIARP)", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "OIARP (observation, interpretation, action, response, plan) captures both the clinical action and the judgment behind it \u2014 the elements malpractice reviewers most often find missing.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_3", "question_id": "course_3_module_3_q05", "question_number": 5, "stem": "A CRNA discovers a factual documentation error two hours after the case. The correct action is to:", "choice_a": "Use the facility's correction/addendum feature, clearly identify the correction, do not obscure the original, and document factually", "choice_b": "Ignore the error to avoid attracting attention", "choice_c": "Backdate the corrected entry to the original time", "choice_d": "Delete the original entry and re-enter the correct information", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Corrections must be transparent, dated, timed, signed, and must not obscure the original record. Deletion or backdating is a serious violation and a well-recognized malpractice red flag.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_3", "question_id": "course_3_module_3_q06", "question_number": 6, "stem": "Which of the following statements about EHR copy-forward is TRUE?", "choice_a": "Copy-forward eliminates the need to sign the note", "choice_b": "Findings should never be copy-forwarded unless the CRNA personally reassessed and confirmed them", "choice_c": "Copy-forward is always acceptable in anesthesia records", "choice_d": "Copy-forward is required by CMS for time savings", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Copy-forward without personal reassessment produces outdated or false findings \u2014 one of the most common EHR-related documentation criticisms in malpractice review.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_3", "question_id": "course_3_module_3_q07", "question_number": 7, "stem": "A patient exsanguinates during a case. Charting was impossible during active resuscitation. The CRNA completes the entry 90 minutes later. The correct approach is to:", "choice_a": "Backdate the entry to match the event time", "choice_b": "Ask a colleague to sign the entry", "choice_c": "Chart as \"late entry,\" note the time the entry is being made and the time the care occurred, and provide an objective factual timeline", "choice_d": "Skip documentation because it is too late", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Late entries are expected in emergencies. They must be clearly identified, dated and timed at the time of entry, and must reference the time care occurred.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_3", "question_id": "course_3_module_3_q08", "question_number": 8, "stem": "The medical record and the institutional incident report differ in that:", "choice_a": "Both should include speculation about fault", "choice_b": "The medical record and the incident report should be identical", "choice_c": "The incident report replaces the medical record in adverse events", "choice_d": "The medical record contains objective clinical facts; the incident report is an internal safety document \u2014 and the medical record should not state that an incident report was filed", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "The medical record is a clinical document; the incident report is an internal safety document. Referencing the incident report in the record can waive certain safety-review protections.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_3", "question_id": "course_3_module_3_q09", "question_number": 9, "stem": "ASA Standards for Basic Anesthetic Monitoring (2020, reaffirmed 2025) require continuous evaluation of:", "choice_a": "Oxygenation, ventilation, circulation, and temperature during all anesthetics", "choice_b": "Only oxygenation and ventilation", "choice_c": "Only blood pressure and heart rate", "choice_d": "Only ECG", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "The ASA standards require continuous evaluation of oxygenation, ventilation, circulation, and temperature during all anesthetics.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_3", "question_id": "course_3_module_3_q10", "question_number": 10, "stem": "Which of the following best reflects AANA Standard 14 (Documentation) when patient-specific circumstances require a deviation from usual practice?", "choice_a": "The deviation must not be documented", "choice_b": "The reason for the modification should be documented in the patient's healthcare record", "choice_c": "The deviation is prohibited", "choice_d": "The deviation should be documented only in the incident report", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "AANA standards recognize that patient-specific circumstances may require modification of usual standards; when a standard is modified, the reason must be documented in the healthcare record.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_3", "question_id": "course_3_module_3_q11", "question_number": 11, "stem": "A malpractice reviewer criticizes an anesthesia record that describes hypotension as \"BP low, treated, patient stable.\" The most defensible replacement note would:", "choice_a": "Add a note that an incident report was filed", "choice_b": "Delete the original entry", "choice_c": "Use the OIARP structure \u2014 objective vitals, interpretation, intervention with rationale, patient response with objective reassessment, and plan", "choice_d": "Say only \"anesthesia complications discussed with team\"", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "OIARP addresses the reviewer's core criticism \u2014 that the record did not show recognition, decision-making, intervention, response, or plan.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_3", "question_id": "course_3_module_3_q12", "question_number": 12, "stem": "A CRNA prints the electronic anesthesia record after a case. It shows an unresolved SpO\u2082 artifact from a probe malfunction that was not explained. In litigation, this is likely to be viewed as:", "choice_a": "Unremarkable, because artifacts are common", "choice_b": "Evidence of high quality of care", "choice_c": "A billing violation", "choice_d": "A gap that should have been explained in the narrative \u2014 unexplained artifacts and gaps are commonly criticized", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Unresolved artifacts, unexplained gaps in monitoring, and missing times are commonly criticized. The CRNA should personalize templated text and explain clinically significant deviations or artifacts.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_3", "question_id": "course_3_module_3_q13", "question_number": 13, "stem": "Which regulation establishes the Condition of Participation for hospital anesthesia services and is the source of the 48-hour preanesthesia and postanesthesia timing rules?", "choice_a": "42 CFR \u00a7482.52", "choice_b": "42 CFR \u00a7482.13", "choice_c": "42 CFR \u00a7482.51", "choice_d": "42 CFR \u00a7482.24", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "42 CFR \u00a7482.52 is the Condition of Participation for anesthesia services. It is the source of the preanesthesia (48 hours before), intraoperative, and postanesthesia (48 hours after) documentation requirements as applied by the CMS Appendix A Interpretive Guidelines.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_3", "question_id": "course_3_module_3_q14", "question_number": 14, "stem": "A patient requests an amendment to the anesthesia record. The CRNA should:", "choice_a": "Ignore the request", "choice_b": "Route the request through the facility health-information-management process per HIPAA \u00a7164.526 and facility policy", "choice_c": "Rewrite the entry to match the patient's version", "choice_d": "Immediately delete the disputed entry", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Patient-requested amendments are handled through the facility HIM process per HIPAA \u00a7164.526. The CRNA should not unilaterally alter the record in response to a patient request.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_3", "question_id": "course_3_module_3_q15", "question_number": 15, "stem": "Which of the following NPDB statements is TRUE?", "choice_a": "The NPDB is a state repository maintained by boards of nursing", "choice_b": "Only physicians are reportable to the NPDB", "choice_c": "The NPDB is a federal repository for medical malpractice payments and certain adverse actions involving healthcare practitioners, including adverse licensure and clinical privileges actions of specified duration", "choice_d": "The NPDB includes only criminal convictions", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "The NPDB is a federal repository administered by HRSA. It captures malpractice payments and adverse licensure, certification, and clinical-privileges actions as defined in the NPDB Guidebook.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_3", "question_id": "course_3_module_3_q16", "question_number": 16, "stem": "During a difficult intubation, defensible documentation should include all of the following EXCEPT:", "choice_a": "Number of attempts and the device used per attempt", "choice_b": "Oxygenation status between attempts", "choice_c": "Notification of help and rescue devices used", "choice_d": "Blame directed at the surgeon for causing the difficult intubation", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "The record should be objective and clinical. Blaming colleagues is inappropriate and damaging in litigation. Documentation should describe attempts, devices, oxygenation, help notification, and rescue actions.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_3", "question_id": "course_3_module_3_q17", "question_number": 17, "stem": "A CRNA transfers care to a colleague for a break. AANA Standard 12 (Transfer of Responsibility for Care) requires documentation that includes:", "choice_a": "The time, receiving clinician, key events, current medications/infusions, outstanding orders, and any acute concerns", "choice_b": "A statement that \"no issues\" occurred", "choice_c": "Only the receiving clinician's name", "choice_d": "Only the time of relief", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "AANA Standard 12 requires communication of information necessary for continued patient care and documentation of the transfer of responsibility \u2014 including time, receiving clinician, and clinically relevant information.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_3", "question_id": "course_3_module_3_q18", "question_number": 18, "stem": "The 21st Century Cures Act and ONC information-blocking rule generally require:", "choice_a": "Charging patients to view their notes", "choice_b": "Prompt patient electronic access to their notes, including anesthesia notes", "choice_c": "Deleting anesthesia notes older than 7 years", "choice_d": "Restricting patient access to anesthesia notes", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "The 21st Century Cures Act and ONC final rule generally require prompt patient electronic access to notes. CRNAs should continue to chart honestly and clinically, while avoiding unnecessarily judgmental language.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_3", "question_id": "course_3_module_3_q19", "question_number": 19, "stem": "A CRNA is asked to sign a preanesthesia note in the EHR that contains contradictory allergy entries. The correct action is to:", "choice_a": "Add a comment stating that the record is unreliable", "choice_b": "Delete both entries", "choice_c": "Reconcile the contradiction before signing", "choice_d": "Sign the note and address the contradiction later", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Contradictions within the same record are a frequent reviewer criticism and can be materially harmful in litigation. The CRNA should reconcile contradictions before signing.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_3", "question_id": "course_3_module_3_q20", "question_number": 20, "stem": "Which of the following is a required element of the postanesthesia evaluation under CMS requirements?", "choice_a": "Anesthesia billing codes", "choice_b": "Discharge medication reconciliation only", "choice_c": "The patient's satisfaction score", "choice_d": "Respiratory function, cardiovascular function, mental status, temperature, pain, nausea/vomiting, and hydration", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "CMS Appendix A specifies the required content of the postanesthesia evaluation, including respiratory, cardiovascular, mental status, temperature, pain, nausea/vomiting, and hydration.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_3", "question_id": "course_3_module_3_q21", "question_number": 21, "stem": "A high-risk preanesthesia finding (e.g., OSA, difficult airway) is documented without a corresponding plan. This is:", "choice_a": "A common malpractice-review concern \u2014 high-risk findings should be tied to a specific plan (e.g., video laryngoscopy, awake fiberoptic contingency, postoperative CPAP, monitored bed)", "choice_b": "Compliant with AANA standards", "choice_c": "Best practice to avoid pre-committing to a plan", "choice_d": "Only a billing issue", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Naming a high-risk finding without a specific plan is a well-recognized documentation weakness. Reviewers expect the record to tie findings to action.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_3", "question_id": "course_3_module_3_q22", "question_number": 22, "stem": "An anesthesia record that has been altered after an adverse event in a way that could be interpreted as concealment is considered:", "choice_a": "Acceptable as long as the CRNA signs it", "choice_b": "One of the most damaging fact patterns in malpractice litigation and independently sanctionable by boards", "choice_c": "Best practice for risk management", "choice_d": "Standard workflow", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Alteration after an adverse event is one of the most damaging fact patterns in litigation and can trigger board action independent of the underlying clinical care.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_3", "question_id": "course_3_module_3_q23", "question_number": 23, "stem": "An anesthesia record contains automated device data that conflicts with the CRNA's narrative note. In review, this typically requires:", "choice_a": "Deleting the conflicting device data", "choice_b": "Ignoring the conflict", "choice_c": "A narrative explanation of the artifact, device malfunction, or intervention that reconciles the two data sources", "choice_d": "Blaming the EHR vendor", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Automated data should be interpreted, not deleted. The narrative should reconcile conflicting data sources \u2014 explaining artifacts, device issues, or interventions.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_3", "question_id": "course_3_module_3_q24", "question_number": 24, "stem": "Which framework is most likely to be cited by a court or expert reviewer as evidence of accepted anesthesia documentation and monitoring expectations for CRNAs?", "choice_a": "Only the CRNA's personal preferences", "choice_b": "Only the surgeon's operative note", "choice_c": "Only the incident report", "choice_d": "AANA Standards for Nurse Anesthesia Practice (2019), ASA Statement on Documentation of Anesthesia Care (2024), CMS 42 CFR \u00a7482.52, and The Joint Commission RC.02.01.01 and RC.01.03.01 \u2014 used together", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Reviewers rely on the combined weight of AANA, ASA, CMS, and Joint Commission requirements \u2014 as well as state law and facility policy \u2014 to define accepted documentation practice.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_3", "question_id": "course_3_module_3_q25", "question_number": 25, "stem": "Which of the following is the single most valuable long-term risk-management tool available to the CRNA?", "choice_a": "An anesthesia record that is accurate, timely, complete, internally consistent, individualized, and demonstrates clinical judgment", "choice_b": "Silence about clinical events", "choice_c": "A brief record with only vital signs", "choice_d": "A record that omits complications to avoid liability", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "An accurate, timely, complete, individualized, and internally consistent record that also demonstrates clinical judgment is the most durable long-term risk-management tool available to the CRNA.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}], "course_3_module_4": [{"module_id": "course_3_module_4", "question_id": "course_3_module_4_q01", "question_number": 1, "stem": "Which statement best describes the relationship between anesthesia standards of care and clinical judgment?", "choice_a": "Standards define minimum expectations; clinical judgment tailors and often exceeds them based on patient, procedure, and setting", "choice_b": "Clinical judgment overrides standards whenever the anesthesia professional believes it is appropriate", "choice_c": "Standards apply only when a facility formally adopts them as policy", "choice_d": "Standards replace clinical judgment because they define exactly what must be done in every case", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Standards define the floor of safe, competent practice. Clinical judgment is required to tailor care to the individual patient, procedure, and setting, and often to exceed the minimum expectation. [Standard]", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_4", "question_id": "course_3_module_4_q02", "question_number": 2, "stem": "How many standards are included in the AANA Standards for Nurse Anesthesia Practice?", "choice_a": "12", "choice_b": "14", "choice_c": "10", "choice_d": "16", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "The AANA Standards for Nurse Anesthesia Practice contain 14 standards addressing patient rights, preanesthesia assessment, planning, informed consent, equipment, plan implementation, patient status, positioning/safety, monitoring/alarms/documentation, infection prevention, transfer of care, quality improvement, culture of safety, and wellness. [Standard]", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_4", "question_id": "course_3_module_4_q03", "question_number": 3, "stem": "Which AANA standard specifically requires that alarms be active and audible when monitoring devices are used?", "choice_a": "Standard 13 (Culture of Safety)", "choice_b": "Standard 7 (Patient Status)", "choice_c": "Standard 9 (Monitoring, Alarms, and Documentation)", "choice_d": "Standard 5 (Equipment)", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "AANA Standard 9 addresses monitoring, alarms, and documentation and specifically requires that alarms be active and audible whenever monitoring devices are used. [Standard]", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_4", "question_id": "course_3_module_4_q04", "question_number": 4, "stem": "The ASA Standards for Basic Anesthetic Monitoring explicitly do NOT apply to which of the following?", "choice_a": "Monitored anesthesia care (MAC)", "choice_b": "General anesthesia", "choice_c": "Regional anesthesia", "choice_d": "Labor analgesia", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "The ASA basic monitoring standards apply to general anesthesia, regional anesthesia, and MAC. They explicitly do not apply to labor analgesia, which is governed by separate obstetric anesthesia guidance. [Standard]", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_4", "question_id": "course_3_module_4_q05", "question_number": 5, "stem": "Under ASA Standard II, arterial blood pressure and heart rate must be determined and evaluated at what minimum interval during anesthesia?", "choice_a": "At least every 5 minutes unless clinically impractical", "choice_b": "At least every 15 minutes", "choice_c": "At least every 10 minutes", "choice_d": "Every minute", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "ASA Standard II requires arterial blood pressure and heart rate to be determined and evaluated at least every 5 minutes unless clinically impractical. [Standard]", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_4", "question_id": "course_3_module_4_q06", "question_number": 6, "stem": "During general anesthesia with an advanced airway, ASA monitoring standards require:", "choice_a": "Only pulse oximetry", "choice_b": "Verification of correct placement and continuous quantitative end-tidal CO\u2082 (capnography) throughout the anesthetic", "choice_c": "ECG monitoring only, unless capnography is available", "choice_d": "Capnography only during the first 30 minutes", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "ASA Standard II requires verification of correct endotracheal or supraglottic airway placement and continuous quantitative end-tidal CO\u2082 monitoring throughout the anesthetic when an advanced airway is used. [Standard]", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_4", "question_id": "course_3_module_4_q07", "question_number": 7, "stem": "Per ASA Standard I, when a brief absence of the primary anesthesia professional is required during a case:", "choice_a": "The case must be paused", "choice_b": "The anesthesia workstation alarms are sufficient replacement", "choice_c": "A qualified individual must assume responsibility, and the arrangement and rationale should be documented", "choice_d": "Unqualified personnel may monitor the patient temporarily", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "ASA Standard I requires that qualified anesthesia personnel be present throughout the anesthetic; if a brief absence is clinically justified, a qualified individual must assume responsibility and the arrangement should be documented. [Standard]", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_4", "question_id": "course_3_module_4_q08", "question_number": 8, "stem": "Which statement best distinguishes a professional 'standard' from a professional 'guideline'?", "choice_a": "Standards apply only to physicians; guidelines apply to nurses", "choice_b": "Standards and guidelines have identical legal weight", "choice_c": "Guidelines are always more current than standards", "choice_d": "Standards describe minimum expected behavior and are enforceable through credentialing and disciplinary action; guidelines are evidence-based recommendations that allow more clinical latitude", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Standards describe minimum expected performance and are enforceable through peer review, credentialing, and licensure. Guidelines are evidence-based recommendations that allow greater latitude for clinical judgment. [Legal]", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_4", "question_id": "course_3_module_4_q09", "question_number": 9, "stem": "A CRNA is deposed in a malpractice case. Which of the following documents is MOST likely to be introduced by an expert witness to define the standard of care owed to the plaintiff?", "choice_a": "The AANA Standards for Nurse Anesthesia Practice and the ASA Standards for Basic Anesthetic Monitoring", "choice_b": "The hospital's marketing brochure", "choice_c": "A general internet search result", "choice_d": "The CRNA's personal continuing education transcript", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "AANA and ASA standards are commonly cited by expert witnesses in malpractice litigation to define the reasonably prudent anesthesia professional's conduct. [Legal]", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_4", "question_id": "course_3_module_4_q10", "question_number": 10, "stem": "A CRNA deviates from a monitoring standard during an emergency. What is the correct approach to documentation?", "choice_a": "Document only that an 'emergency occurred'", "choice_b": "Document the specific standard affected, the clinical reason for deviation, and any mitigating actions taken", "choice_c": "Wait 30 days and then document from memory", "choice_d": "Do not document the deviation to avoid legal exposure", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Both AANA and ASA standards acknowledge that documented, justified deviations are consistent with standards-based care. Deviations should be documented with clinical rationale and mitigating actions. [Documentation]", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_4", "question_id": "course_3_module_4_q11", "question_number": 11, "stem": "Which federal regulation establishes anesthesia service requirements for Medicare-participating hospitals?", "choice_a": "45 CFR \u00a7164.502", "choice_b": "42 USC \u00a71395dd (EMTALA)", "choice_c": "42 CFR \u00a7482.52", "choice_d": "21 CFR \u00a71301.11", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "42 CFR \u00a7482.52 is the Anesthesia Services Condition of Participation for hospitals. \u00a7485.639 covers critical access hospitals; \u00a7416.42 covers ambulatory surgical centers. [Regulatory]", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_4", "question_id": "course_3_module_4_q12", "question_number": 12, "stem": "Which of the following is NOT among the four physiologic domains addressed by ASA Standard II (continuous physiologic evaluation)?", "choice_a": "Oxygenation", "choice_b": "Circulation", "choice_c": "Ventilation", "choice_d": "Bispectral index (BIS)", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "ASA Standard II addresses oxygenation, ventilation, circulation, and temperature. Bispectral index (BIS) is not a required element of basic anesthetic monitoring. [Standard]", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_4", "question_id": "course_3_module_4_q13", "question_number": 13, "stem": "A CRNA is preparing to add anesthesia services at an office-based plastic surgery practice. Which of the following is the MOST complete pre-service verification list?", "choice_a": "Confirm governance, patient selection criteria, staffing, equipment, recovery capability, transfer agreement, MH preparedness when triggering agents are used, controlled substance management, infection prevention, and backup power/oxygen", "choice_b": "Verify the facility's marketing plan", "choice_c": "Confirm oxygen supply only", "choice_d": "Confirm only the surgeon's board certification", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Per AANA Office Based Anesthesia guidance and ASA Statement on Office-Based Anesthesia (2024), CRNAs should verify governance, patient selection, staffing, equipment, recovery capability, transfer agreement, MH preparedness, controlled-substance management, infection prevention, and backup power/oxygen. [Application]", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_4", "question_id": "course_3_module_4_q14", "question_number": 14, "stem": "Which of the following is the MOST accurate description of the ASA monitoring standards' relationship to nonoperating room anesthesia (NORA)?", "choice_a": "Only anesthesiologists may practice in NORA locations", "choice_b": "ASA basic monitoring standards apply to NORA locations; environmental considerations (space, power, communication, rescue) add operational requirements per the ASA Statement on Nonoperating Room Anesthetizing Locations", "choice_c": "NORA sites are exempt from capnography requirements", "choice_d": "ASA monitoring standards do not apply outside the operating room", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "The ASA basic monitoring standards apply to NORA. The ASA Statement on Nonoperating Room Anesthetizing Locations (2023) adds environmental and operational expectations regarding space, oxygen supply, suction, power, lighting, communication, and rescue capability. [Application]", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_4", "question_id": "course_3_module_4_q15", "question_number": 15, "stem": "Per the 2024 ASRA/ESRA Fourth Consensus Practice Advisory on local anesthetic systemic toxicity (LAST), the initial intravenous lipid emulsion bolus for LAST treatment is:", "choice_a": "5 mL/kg of 20% lipid emulsion over 10 minutes", "choice_b": "0.5 mL/kg of 10% lipid emulsion", "choice_c": "1.5 mL/kg of 20% lipid emulsion over 2\u20133 minutes, followed by 0.25 mL/kg/min infusion", "choice_d": "Lipid emulsion is not recommended for LAST", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Per the 2024 ASRA/ESRA update, initial treatment includes 20% intravenous lipid emulsion 1.5 mL/kg bolus over 2\u20133 minutes, followed by infusion at 0.25 mL/kg/min; bolus may be repeated for persistent cardiovascular instability. [Application]", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_4", "question_id": "course_3_module_4_q16", "question_number": 16, "stem": "During regional anesthesia, minimum recommended post-injection monitoring for LAST recognition is:", "choice_a": "5 minutes", "choice_b": "15 minutes", "choice_c": "1 hour, but only for high-risk patients", "choice_d": "At least 30 minutes with continuous physiologic monitoring in a monitored setting", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Per current ASRA/ESRA guidance, patients should be monitored for at least 30 minutes after the final local anesthetic injection with continuous physiologic monitoring in a monitored setting, because LAST may present with delayed onset. [Application]", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_4", "question_id": "course_3_module_4_q17", "question_number": 17, "stem": "Which statement about obstetric anesthesia is MOST consistent with current AANA and ASA guidance?", "choice_a": "ASA basic monitoring standards are not intended to define labor analgesia monitoring; obstetric-specific guidance and facility protocols apply, with core principles of assessment, vigilance, documentation, and emergency readiness maintained", "choice_b": "CRNAs may not provide labor analgesia in any state", "choice_c": "ASA basic monitoring standards fully define labor analgesia monitoring requirements", "choice_d": "Neuraxial techniques do not require informed consent", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "ASA basic monitoring standards explicitly do not apply to labor analgesia. AANA Obstetric Anesthesia and Analgesia Practice Considerations (2023) and setting-specific guidance govern, while core standards principles remain in effect. [Application]", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_4", "question_id": "course_3_module_4_q18", "question_number": 18, "stem": "A CRNA is called to a mass casualty event in which conventional anesthesia care cannot be maintained. Which of the following BEST describes standards-based practice under crisis standards of care?", "choice_a": "The CRNA should refuse to work until conventional standards can be restored", "choice_b": "Crisis standards allow triage-based resource allocation, altered staffing, and modified documentation, but core safety principles and professional accountability remain; deviations should be documented when feasible", "choice_c": "All professional accountability is suspended", "choice_d": "Documentation is not required during crisis events", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Crisis standards of care permit operational modifications under declared crisis conditions but do not eliminate professional accountability. Core safety and, when feasible, contemporaneous documentation of deviations should continue. [Application]", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_4", "question_id": "course_3_module_4_q19", "question_number": 19, "stem": "A facility's anesthesia policy adopts AANA and ASA standards by reference. What is the legal effect for a CRNA credentialed at that facility?", "choice_a": "Only the medical staff bylaws are enforceable", "choice_b": "The standards apply only to physicians, not to CRNAs", "choice_c": "The facility policy makes AANA and ASA standards locally enforceable through credentialing and disciplinary action", "choice_d": "The standards remain purely aspirational because they are external to the facility", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "When a facility policy adopts external standards, those standards become locally binding through credentialing, peer review, and disciplinary processes. [Legal]", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_4", "question_id": "course_3_module_4_q20", "question_number": 20, "stem": "Which of the following is a specific requirement of ASA Standard I regarding qualified anesthesia personnel?", "choice_a": "Personnel may leave the room during stable maintenance phases with no documentation required", "choice_b": "Personnel must hold board certification in anesthesiology", "choice_c": "Only physicians may be present during general anesthesia", "choice_d": "Qualified anesthesia personnel remain present in the room throughout general anesthesia, regional anesthesia, and monitored anesthesia care, unless a temporary absence is clinically justified with safe monitoring arrangements", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "ASA Standard I requires that qualified anesthesia personnel be present in the room throughout general anesthesia, regional anesthesia, and MAC unless a brief absence is clinically justified with safe monitoring arrangements. [Standard]", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_4", "question_id": "course_3_module_4_q21", "question_number": 21, "stem": "AANA Standards 13 (Culture of Safety) and 14 (Wellness) were elevated as formal standards because:", "choice_a": "Human factors \u2014 including fatigue, stress, burnout, and impairment \u2014 measurably degrade patient safety, and organizational culture affects error reporting, systems learning, and outcomes", "choice_b": "Wellness and safety culture are optional considerations only", "choice_c": "These standards apply only to trainees", "choice_d": "The AANA needed to lengthen the standards document", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Human factors research and patient safety literature demonstrate that clinician wellness and safety culture directly affect performance, reporting, and outcomes, which supported formal elevation of these domains within the AANA standards. [Standard]", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_4", "question_id": "course_3_module_4_q22", "question_number": 22, "stem": "Which of the following is the correct interpretation of the terms 'continual' and 'continuous' in the ASA monitoring standards?", "choice_a": "'Continual' means uninterrupted electronic monitoring only", "choice_b": "'Continual' means repeated regularly and frequently in steady rapid succession, while 'continuous' means prolonged without interruption at any time", "choice_c": "The terms are interchangeable", "choice_d": "'Continuous' means every 15 minutes", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Per the ASA monitoring standards, 'continual' means repeated regularly and frequently in steady rapid succession, and 'continuous' means prolonged without any interruption at any time. Pulse oximetry is continuous; noninvasive blood pressure is continual. [Standard]", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_4", "question_id": "course_3_module_4_q23", "question_number": 23, "stem": "A CRNA discovers that the pulse oximeter in a NORA suite is not functioning immediately before beginning a MAC case. What is the correct response consistent with AANA and ASA standards?", "choice_a": "Substitute capnography for pulse oximetry indefinitely", "choice_b": "Proceed with the case and monitor blood oxygenation clinically", "choice_c": "Delay the case until a functional pulse oximeter is available; AANA Standard 5 (Equipment) and ASA basic monitoring require functioning pulse oximetry for these anesthetics", "choice_d": "Ask the proceduralist to determine whether to proceed", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Both AANA Standard 5 (Equipment) and ASA basic monitoring standards require pulse oximetry for general anesthesia, regional anesthesia, and MAC. The case should be delayed until functional monitoring is available. [Application]", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_4", "question_id": "course_3_module_4_q24", "question_number": 24, "stem": "Which of the following documents specifically addresses accreditation expectations for anesthesia care in U.S. hospitals?", "choice_a": "USPSTF recommendations", "choice_b": "OSHA General Duty Clause", "choice_c": "FDA Center for Devices and Radiological Health guidance", "choice_d": "The Joint Commission Comprehensive Accreditation Manual (PC.03.01.01 through PC.03.01.07)", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "The Joint Commission's Comprehensive Accreditation Manual for Hospitals contains anesthesia care standards PC.03.01.01 through PC.03.01.07 covering preanesthesia assessment, informed consent, monitoring, and postanesthesia care. [Regulatory]", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_4", "question_id": "course_3_module_4_q25", "question_number": 25, "stem": "For a routine general anesthetic, which of the following is the MOST accurate summary of the CRNA's alignment with both AANA and ASA standards?", "choice_a": "Meet the ASA basic monitoring floor for oxygenation, ventilation, circulation, and temperature; and satisfy AANA standards across assessment, planning, consent, equipment, plan implementation, monitoring/alarms/documentation, transfer, and related professional responsibilities", "choice_b": "Meet the AANA standards and disregard the ASA standards because they were written for physicians", "choice_c": "Follow ASA only when the case is billed to Medicare", "choice_d": "Follow whichever framework the surgeon prefers", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "AANA and ASA standards are complementary. CRNAs are expected to meet the ASA monitoring floor and the full AANA professional practice framework for every routine general anesthetic. [Standard]", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}], "course_3_module_5": [{"module_id": "course_3_module_5", "question_id": "course_3_module_5_q01", "question_number": 1, "stem": "Per NCC MERP and ISMP, a medication error is best defined as:", "choice_a": "Any preventable event that may cause or lead to inappropriate medication use or patient harm while the medication is in the control of a healthcare professional, patient, or consumer", "choice_b": "Any patient-reported side effect after emergence", "choice_c": "Any deviation from a written medication order that is discovered after the case", "choice_d": "Any adverse drug reaction that occurs during anesthesia care", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "NCC MERP defines a medication error as any preventable event that may cause or lead to inappropriate medication use or patient harm while the medication is in the control of a healthcare professional, patient, or consumer. This encompasses both errors that reach the patient and those intercepted (near misses).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_5", "question_id": "course_3_module_5_q02", "question_number": 2, "stem": "A CRNA intends to draw up ondansetron but grabs phenylephrine because the two syringes are similarly positioned on the anesthesia machine. Which classification best applies?", "choice_a": "Adverse drug event unrelated to error", "choice_b": "Slip \u2014 failure of execution despite the correct plan", "choice_c": "Reckless behavior under just-culture principles", "choice_d": "Mistake \u2014 failure of planning or knowledge", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "A slip is a failure of execution despite the correct plan, typically caused by habit capture, distraction, or workspace design. A mistake is a failure of planning or knowledge (the plan itself is wrong). Slips are best addressed with system engineering.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_5", "question_id": "course_3_module_5_q03", "question_number": 3, "stem": "Which of the following is a latent error rather than an active error?", "choice_a": "The CRNA injects rocuronium instead of cefazolin", "choice_b": "The CRNA administers medication to the wrong patient", "choice_c": "Look-alike vial caps are stored together on the anesthesia cart without a barcode reader", "choice_d": "The CRNA programs an infusion rate at 10 times the intended value", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Latent errors are hidden system weaknesses (look-alike storage, missing barcode technology, understaffing) that make active errors more likely. The remaining choices are active errors at the point of care.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_5", "question_id": "course_3_module_5_q04", "question_number": 4, "stem": "Which of the following medications is not on the 2024 ISMP List of High-Alert Medications in Acute Care Settings?", "choice_a": "IV opioids and epidural opioids", "choice_b": "Concentrated potassium chloride", "choice_c": "Neuromuscular blocking agents", "choice_d": "Acetaminophen 650 mg oral", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "The 2024 ISMP High-Alert list includes NMBAs, IV and epidural opioids, concentrated electrolytes, insulin, anticoagulants, sedatives/hypnotics for procedural use, and local anesthetics \u22650.5% or given epidurally. Standard oral acetaminophen is not on the list.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_5", "question_id": "course_3_module_5_q05", "question_number": 5, "stem": "Which look-alike/sound-alike pattern was the focus of a 2024 APSF Newsletter multistakeholder alert about a recurrent wrong-drug, wrong-route error in anesthesia?", "choice_a": "Tranexamic acid mistaken for bupivacaine or ropivacaine during neuraxial anesthesia", "choice_b": "Ephedrine mistaken for epinephrine", "choice_c": "Heparin 10 units/mL mistaken for heparin 10,000 units/mL", "choice_d": "Vecuronium mistaken for Versed", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "The June 2024 APSF Newsletter published a multistakeholder analysis of recurrent inadvertent neuraxial injection of tranexamic acid (TXA) in place of bupivacaine or ropivacaine, an event with mortality of 40\u201350% in published series.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_5", "question_id": "course_3_module_5_q06", "question_number": 6, "stem": "Which APSF acronym best summarizes the four pillars of perioperative medication safety reaffirmed at the 2024 Stoelting Consensus Conference?", "choice_a": "SAFE \u2014 Screening, Awareness, Feedback, Education", "choice_b": "STPC \u2014 Standardization, Technology, Pharmacy/prefilled, Culture", "choice_c": "MEDS \u2014 Monitor, Evaluate, Document, Standardize", "choice_d": "CHECK \u2014 Confirm, Handoff, Educate, Coordinate, Know", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "APSF frames medication safety around Standardization (concentrations, labels), Technology (BCMA, smart pumps, EHR), Pharmacy/prefilled syringes for high-risk drugs, and Culture (reporting, teamwork, disclosure).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_5", "question_id": "course_3_module_5_q07", "question_number": 7, "stem": "Per current ASRA guidance, the first dose of 20% lipid emulsion for adult LAST is:", "choice_a": "5 mL/kg bolus, followed by 1 mL/kg/min infusion", "choice_b": "3 mL/kg bolus, no infusion", "choice_c": "1.5 mL/kg bolus, followed by 0.25 mL/kg/min infusion", "choice_d": "10 mL/kg bolus over 10 minutes", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "ASRA's 2020 (reaffirmed 2024) LAST checklist recommends 20% lipid emulsion 1.5 mL/kg IV bolus over 2\u20133 minutes followed by 0.25 mL/kg/min infusion, with bolus and infusion adjustments per patient response.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_5", "question_id": "course_3_module_5_q08", "question_number": 8, "stem": "Which epinephrine dose does ASRA recommend for hemodynamic support during LAST in adults?", "choice_a": "Vasopressin 40 units IV push", "choice_b": "No epinephrine \u2014 only lipid emulsion", "choice_c": "Standard ACLS doses of 1 mg IV every 3\u20135 minutes", "choice_d": "Reduced doses of 10\u2013100 mcg IV boluses", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "ASRA recommends smaller, incremental epinephrine boluses (10\u2013100 mcg in adults) during LAST because standard ACLS doses can worsen arrhythmias and impair lipid rescue. Vasopressin is specifically to be avoided.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_5", "question_id": "course_3_module_5_q09", "question_number": 9, "stem": "Which of the following is required by Joint Commission National Patient Safety Goal 03.04.01?", "choice_a": "Labeling all medications, medication containers, and other solutions on and off the sterile field in perioperative and other procedural settings", "choice_b": "Elimination of manual medication preparation in all facilities", "choice_c": "Barcode scanning of every medication in every OR", "choice_d": "Independent double checks of every anesthesia medication", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "NPSG 03.04.01 requires labeling of all medications, medication containers, and other solutions on and off the sterile field in perioperative and procedural settings \u2014 a standard designed specifically to prevent wrong-drug and wrong-route events.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_5", "question_id": "course_3_module_5_q10", "question_number": 10, "stem": "Which four elements must a plaintiff establish in a CRNA medication-error negligence claim?", "choice_a": "Intent, breach, causation, damages", "choice_b": "Duty, breach, causation, damages", "choice_c": "Duty, foreseeability, mitigation, damages", "choice_d": "Duty, malice, causation, punitive damages", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Every U.S. jurisdiction requires the classical four elements of negligence: duty, breach of the standard of care, causation (actual and proximate), and compensable damages.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_5", "question_id": "course_3_module_5_q11", "question_number": 11, "stem": "Under Just Culture principles, which category of behavior warrants consoling the individual and redesigning the system rather than disciplinary action?", "choice_a": "Reckless behavior", "choice_b": "At-risk behavior", "choice_c": "Human error (inadvertent slip, lapse, or mistake)", "choice_d": "Willful misconduct", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Marx's Just Culture model calls for consoling the individual and repairing the system after human error; coaching for at-risk behavior; and remedial or disciplinary action only for reckless behavior (conscious disregard of substantial, unjustifiable risk).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_5", "question_id": "course_3_module_5_q12", "question_number": 12, "stem": "The 2022 criminal conviction of nurse RaDonda Vaught involved inadvertent administration of which medication in place of the intended Versed (midazolam)?", "choice_a": "Vasopressin", "choice_b": "Vancomycin", "choice_c": "Verapamil", "choice_d": "Vecuronium", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Ms. Vaught inadvertently administered vecuronium (a neuromuscular blocker) instead of Versed (midazolam) after overriding the automated dispensing cabinet safety features. Nursing and safety organizations widely warned that the criminal prosecution would suppress voluntary reporting.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_5", "question_id": "course_3_module_5_q13", "question_number": 13, "stem": "A CRNA discovers that the propofol infusion line was not connected to the patient's IV during a paralyzed general anesthetic. The patient reports intraoperative awareness with recall. Which combination of preventive measures is most directly indicated?", "choice_a": "Verify the infusion line to the patient before induction and monitor end-tidal anesthetic concentration or processed EEG when infusion-only techniques are used", "choice_b": "Perform routine intraoperative naloxone administration", "choice_c": "Reduce opioid dose in future cases", "choice_d": "Discontinue the use of propofol infusions", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Line-to-patient verification and continuous monitoring of anesthetic depth (end-tidal anesthetic gas or processed EEG) are the recognized safeguards against unrecognized delivery failures during infusion-based general anesthesia \u2014 the classic setup for awareness in a paralyzed patient.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_5", "question_id": "course_3_module_5_q14", "question_number": 14, "stem": "Which monitoring parameter, per current AANA and ASA standards, is required for all patients receiving moderate or deep sedation, including MAC?", "choice_a": "Invasive arterial blood pressure", "choice_b": "Continuous end-tidal CO2 (capnography)", "choice_c": "Transcutaneous oxygen tension", "choice_d": "Bispectral index (BIS)", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Current AANA and ASA standards require continuous capnography for all moderate/deep sedation and general anesthesia to detect hypoventilation, obstruction, and apnea earlier than pulse oximetry alone.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_5", "question_id": "course_3_module_5_q15", "question_number": 15, "stem": "During a wrong-drug injection into a neuraxial catheter, which of the following is the most important immediate step to prevent recurrence in the future?", "choice_a": "Require an anesthesiologist to inject all neuraxial medications", "choice_b": "File a lawsuit against the vial manufacturer", "choice_c": "Never inject a medication into a neuraxial catheter that the CRNA has not personally verified by label and, when available, by barcode scan", "choice_d": "Discontinue the use of neuraxial anesthesia", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Personal label verification (and barcode scan when available) immediately before neuraxial injection is the single most effective safeguard against catastrophic wrong-drug or wrong-route events, per APSF and ISMP.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_5", "question_id": "course_3_module_5_q16", "question_number": 16, "stem": "Which of the following is the most appropriate documentation practice immediately after a medication error?", "choice_a": "Chart that an incident report was filed and describe the suspected root cause", "choice_b": "Amend prior chart entries to reflect the corrected clinical picture", "choice_c": "Delay documentation until legal counsel reviews the chart", "choice_d": "Document a timely, objective, factual account of the medication, assessment, interventions, notifications, and patient response \u2014 and file the incident report separately", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Documentation should be timely, objective, and factual. Incident reports are separate from the medical record and generally should not be referenced in the chart. Prior entries must never be altered or backdated; late entries must be labeled as such.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_5", "question_id": "course_3_module_5_q17", "question_number": 17, "stem": "Which external reporting system is most appropriate for a CRNA who identifies a defect in vial labeling that contributed to a wrong-drug event?", "choice_a": "FDA MedWatch", "choice_b": "Facility marketing department", "choice_c": "AANA membership hotline", "choice_d": "State department of transportation", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "FDA MedWatch is the appropriate voluntary reporting channel for serious adverse events and product-quality problems (including labeling and packaging defects). ISMP MERP is a complementary, ISMP-operated program.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_5", "question_id": "course_3_module_5_q18", "question_number": 18, "stem": "Which of the following is the correct order of confirmation steps at the anesthesia workstation immediately before administering a medication?", "choice_a": "Confirm the medication only with the circulating nurse after administration", "choice_b": "Read the label when the vial is retrieved, again when the medication is drawn up, and again immediately before administration \u2014 with barcode scan when available", "choice_c": "Draw up, label, administer, then read the label", "choice_d": "Rely on vial-cap color, then administer", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Reading the label three times (retrieval, draw-up, administration) plus barcode scan when available is the standard risk-reduction bundle recommended by APSF, ISMP, and AANA.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_5", "question_id": "course_3_module_5_q19", "question_number": 19, "stem": "Which quantitative train-of-four ratio is the current recommended threshold for tracheal extubation to minimize residual neuromuscular blockade?", "choice_a": "\u2265 1.2", "choice_b": "\u2265 0.7", "choice_c": "\u2265 0.9", "choice_d": "\u2265 0.5", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Current APSF, ASA, and AANA guidance recommends quantitative TOF monitoring with a ratio \u2265 0.9 before extubation to minimize residual neuromuscular blockade and postoperative respiratory complications.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_5", "question_id": "course_3_module_5_q20", "question_number": 20, "stem": "Which of the following medications is not reversed by sugammadex?", "choice_a": "Rocuronium", "choice_b": "Vecuronium", "choice_c": "None \u2014 sugammadex reverses all three aminosteroid NMBAs", "choice_d": "Cisatracurium", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Sugammadex encapsulates aminosteroid NMBAs (rocuronium, vecuronium, and to a lesser extent pancuronium) but does not reverse benzylisoquinolinium agents such as cisatracurium. Reversal choice must match the NMBA class and the current depth of block.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_5", "question_id": "course_3_module_5_q21", "question_number": 21, "stem": "A structured handoff between the CRNA and the PACU nurse should include, at minimum:", "choice_a": "Medications given, infusions currently running, allergies, patient response, and anticipated next doses", "choice_b": "The CRNA's personal opinion of the case", "choice_c": "The surgeon's preferences only", "choice_d": "The billing code for anesthesia services", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "ISMP Canada's 2024 PACU multi-incident analysis and the AANA Standards emphasize structured handoffs that include medications given, infusions running, allergies, patient response, and anticipated next doses. I-PASS or SBAR formats are recommended.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_5", "question_id": "course_3_module_5_q22", "question_number": 22, "stem": "Vicarious liability in a CRNA medication-error case may extend to which of the following parties?", "choice_a": "The medication manufacturer under products-liability theories when packaging design contributed to the error", "choice_b": "All of the above, depending on facts and employment structure", "choice_c": "The facility and the anesthesia group", "choice_d": "A medically directing anesthesiologist under TEFRA rules", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Vicarious liability may reach the facility, group, medically directing anesthesiologist, and (occasionally) manufacturer, depending on employment status, medical-direction structure, credentialing, and packaging design contributions.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_5", "question_id": "course_3_module_5_q23", "question_number": 23, "stem": "Which of the following statements about the Patient Safety and Quality Improvement Act of 2005 (PSQIA) is most accurate?", "choice_a": "It eliminates civil liability for medication errors", "choice_b": "It requires public reporting of every medication error", "choice_c": "It provides federal confidentiality and privilege protection for patient-safety work product submitted through a certified Patient Safety Organization (PSO)", "choice_d": "It preempts all state peer-review protection statutes", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "PSQIA (42 U.S.C. \u00a7\u00a7299b-21\u2013299b-26) creates federal confidentiality and privilege for patient-safety work product submitted through certified PSOs. State peer-review protections continue to apply and are not preempted.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_5", "question_id": "course_3_module_5_q24", "question_number": 24, "stem": "Which of the following best captures the AANA and APSF position on nonpunitive medication-error reporting?", "choice_a": "Reporting is optional and generally discouraged for individual practitioners", "choice_b": "Reporting should be limited to events resulting in patient death", "choice_c": "Only pharmacy staff should report medication errors", "choice_d": "Voluntary reporting of near misses and actual errors is essential for system learning; punitive responses to human error suppress reporting and worsen patient safety", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "AANA, APSF, ISMP, and Joint Commission consistently endorse voluntary, nonpunitive reporting of near misses and errors as the foundation of medication-safety improvement. Punitive responses to human error suppress reporting and are associated with worse safety outcomes.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_5", "question_id": "course_3_module_5_q25", "question_number": 25, "stem": "A CRNA in an outpatient endoscopy suite plans MAC with propofol and fentanyl for a high-risk patient with obesity, OSA, and renal dysfunction. Which of the following most reduces medication-related liability exposure?", "choice_a": "Verify the medically directing anesthesiologist's supervisory presence per policy, use continuous capnography, ensure immediate availability of naloxone and airway rescue equipment, and document the plan and monitoring", "choice_b": "Reserve intubation for cardiac arrest", "choice_c": "Skip continuous capnography to reduce alarms", "choice_d": "Rely on pulse oximetry alone because MAC does not require capnography", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "High-risk MAC patients require full standard monitoring (including capnography), clear supervisory presence per medical-direction rules, immediate rescue readiness (naloxone, airway equipment), and complete documentation \u2014 the safeguards most often missing in published sedation-related closed-claim cases.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}], "course_3_module_6": [{"module_id": "course_3_module_6", "question_id": "course_3_module_6_q01", "question_number": 1, "stem": "A malpractice (professional negligence) claim against a CRNA generally requires proof of all of the following elements EXCEPT:", "choice_a": "A criminal-law mens rea (intent to harm)", "choice_b": "Breach of the standard of care", "choice_c": "Duty", "choice_d": "Actual and proximate causation linking the breach to the injury", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Professional negligence is a civil claim and does not require criminal intent. The four required elements are duty, breach, causation, and damages.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_6", "question_id": "course_3_module_6_q02", "question_number": 2, "stem": "In a CRNA malpractice case, the \u201cstandard of care\u201d is best described as:", "choice_a": "The care described in any single textbook chapter", "choice_b": "What a reasonably prudent anesthesia provider of similar training would do under similar circumstances, defined by professional standards, facility policies, and jurisdictional rules", "choice_c": "Whatever the facility's chief anesthesiologist personally prefers", "choice_d": "The absolute best possible care available anywhere", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Standard of care is the reasonably prudent anesthesia provider standard, defined by AANA/ASA/ASRA/APSF standards, policies, and jurisdictional rules \u2014 not perfection.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_6", "question_id": "course_3_module_6_q03", "question_number": 3, "stem": "A CRNA delivers anesthesia in accordance with all applicable standards, but the patient suffers a rare pulmonary embolism and dies. Which element of malpractice is most clearly absent?", "choice_a": "Duty", "choice_b": "Causation only", "choice_c": "Breach", "choice_d": "Damages", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "If the CRNA met the standard of care, there is no breach. Duty and damages exist; causation would be moot without a breach.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_6", "question_id": "course_3_module_6_q04", "question_number": 4, "stem": "According to the AANA Foundation Malpractice Closed Claims Study (Jordan & Quraishi, 2015), the majority of CRNA-associated damaging events reviewed occurred during which phase of care?", "choice_a": "Preadmission testing", "choice_b": "Postoperative discharge", "choice_c": "Preoperative evaluation", "choice_d": "Intra-anesthesia (intraoperative/intraprocedural)", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "The AANA Foundation closed-claims study found that most damaging events occurred intra-anesthesia, with airway, regional, medication, and general anesthesia events predominant.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_6", "question_id": "course_3_module_6_q05", "question_number": 5, "stem": "Tracy and colleagues (Health Affairs Scholar, 2026) analyzed National Practitioner Data Bank malpractice payments for anesthesia-related claims. Their principal finding was:", "choice_a": "CRNA malpractice payment rates and severe-injury probabilities are broadly comparable to those for physician anesthesiologists when adjusted for practice volume", "choice_b": "CRNAs are almost never named in anesthesia claims", "choice_c": "CRNA malpractice payment rates are dramatically higher than those for physician anesthesiologists", "choice_d": "Physician anesthesiologists never face malpractice suits", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Tracy et al. reported broadly comparable malpractice payment patterns and severe-injury probabilities between CRNAs and physician anesthesiologists after adjustment.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_6", "question_id": "course_3_module_6_q06", "question_number": 6, "stem": "Which category has consistently been the largest source of death and severe brain injury in anesthesia closed-claims analyses?", "choice_a": "Positioning-related pressure injury", "choice_b": "Airway and respiratory events (difficult intubation, esophageal intubation, delayed rescue, PACU respiratory depression)", "choice_c": "Dental injury", "choice_d": "IV infiltration", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Airway and respiratory events remain the single largest driver of catastrophic anesthesia claims in both ASA and AANA closed-claims data.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_6", "question_id": "course_3_module_6_q07", "question_number": 7, "stem": "Under the doctrine of respondeat superior:", "choice_a": "The patient is presumed responsible for anesthesia outcomes", "choice_b": "The federal government indemnifies all healthcare providers", "choice_c": "An employer is generally responsible for the negligent acts of employees that occur within the scope of employment, though the employee remains individually liable", "choice_d": "The state board of nursing takes responsibility for CRNA errors", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Respondeat superior imputes employee negligence to the employer when the act was within the scope of employment; the employee remains individually liable.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_6", "question_id": "course_3_module_6_q08", "question_number": 8, "stem": "A hospital is sued for negligently credentialing a CRNA whose history of remedial action was not verified during the credentialing process. The legal theory most directly applicable to the hospital is:", "choice_a": "Respondeat superior", "choice_b": "Captain of the ship", "choice_c": "Res ipsa loquitur only", "choice_d": "Corporate negligence", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Corporate negligence describes the facility's independent duty to credential competent providers, adopt reasonable policies, and monitor safety.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_6", "question_id": "course_3_module_6_q09", "question_number": 9, "stem": "A 1099 independent-contractor CRNA is sued after an anesthesia event. Which of the following is the most accurate coverage statement?", "choice_a": "The CRNA's personal professional-liability policy is typically the primary source of defense and indemnity; contractor status limits respondeat superior protection", "choice_b": "Federal law provides mandatory coverage", "choice_c": "Only the facility is liable", "choice_d": "Employer respondeat superior automatically covers the CRNA", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "1099 contractors typically depend on personal coverage; respondeat superior generally does not apply.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_6", "question_id": "course_3_module_6_q10", "question_number": 10, "stem": "Occurrence-based professional-liability coverage is triggered by:", "choice_a": "The date the claim is reported to the carrier", "choice_b": "The date the clinical event occurred, regardless of when the claim is later filed", "choice_c": "The date of the initial deposition", "choice_d": "The date the CRNA's employment ends", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Occurrence coverage attaches based on when the care event occurred; the policy responds even if the claim is filed later.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_6", "question_id": "course_3_module_6_q11", "question_number": 11, "stem": "Claims-made coverage typically requires which additional protection when a CRNA leaves an employer, retires, or switches carriers?", "choice_a": "A federal tort waiver", "choice_b": "Waiver from the state board of nursing", "choice_c": "Tail coverage (extended reporting endorsement) or matching prior-acts (nose) coverage from the new carrier", "choice_d": "A new state license", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Claims-made policies cover only claims reported while the policy is active; tail or prior-acts coverage is needed to protect care delivered during the policy period.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_6", "question_id": "course_3_module_6_q12", "question_number": 12, "stem": "A CRNA's claims-made policy has a $1M per-claim / $3M aggregate limit with defense costs paid inside the limits. If defense costs on a single claim reach $400,000 before settlement, how much of the per-claim limit remains available for indemnity?", "choice_a": "None", "choice_b": "$3,000,000", "choice_c": "$1,000,000", "choice_d": "$600,000", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "With defense costs inside the limits, defense expenses reduce the amount available for settlement dollar for dollar: $1,000,000 \u2212 $400,000 = $600,000.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_6", "question_id": "course_3_module_6_q13", "question_number": 13, "stem": "A \u201chammer clause\u201d in a professional-liability policy:", "choice_a": "May shift financial risk to the CRNA if the CRNA refuses to consent to a settlement the insurer recommends", "choice_b": "Requires the CRNA to purchase tail coverage", "choice_c": "Prohibits the CRNA from testifying at deposition", "choice_d": "Automatically increases coverage limits after five claim-free years", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "A hammer clause conditions the insurer's continued defense or indemnity on the CRNA's consent to settle; refusing settlement can shift risk to the CRNA.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_6", "question_id": "course_3_module_6_q14", "question_number": 14, "stem": "A commonly seen baseline professional-liability limit for CRNAs is:", "choice_a": "$25 million per claim / $75 million aggregate", "choice_b": "$1 million per claim / $3 million aggregate", "choice_c": "$250,000 per claim / $750,000 aggregate", "choice_d": "$100,000 per claim / $300,000 aggregate", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "$1M/$3M is widely cited as a baseline minimum; higher limits are typically appropriate for independent, locum, office-based, or high-acuity practice.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_6", "question_id": "course_3_module_6_q15", "question_number": 15, "stem": "Which of the following is a defensible reason to purchase individual (supplemental) coverage in addition to employer coverage?", "choice_a": "Employer policies always cover moonlighting and expert-witness work", "choice_b": "Individual coverage eliminates the need for facility credentialing", "choice_c": "Individual coverage may provide separate legal representation, license-defense benefits, and greater control over settlement decisions, plus protection for non-assigned work", "choice_d": "Individual coverage removes the CRNA from NPDB reporting", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Individual coverage adds independent counsel, license-defense, consent-to-settle rights, and protection for work outside employment.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_6", "question_id": "course_3_module_6_q16", "question_number": 16, "stem": "A CRNA is deposed after a claim is filed. Which statement is the best guidance?", "choice_a": "Never review the anesthesia record before the deposition", "choice_b": "Volunteer all relevant clinical opinions, even when not asked", "choice_c": "Speculate when unsure \u2014 jurors expect certainty", "choice_d": "Answer only the question asked; do not speculate; take time to think; correct prior misstatements; and prepare with defense counsel", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Standard deposition guidance is to listen, answer only the question asked, avoid speculation, and prepare with defense counsel.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_6", "question_id": "course_3_module_6_q17", "question_number": 17, "stem": "Which of the following documentation practices most reliably supports the defense of a CRNA case?", "choice_a": "Contemporaneous, factual, internally consistent charting that reconciles with monitor data and reflects the actual supervision model", "choice_b": "Copying prior anesthetic notes forward to save time", "choice_c": "Using vague summary language such as \u201cpatient tolerated well\u201d after critical events", "choice_d": "Retrospectively rewriting the anesthesia record to add clarifying detail after litigation begins", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Contemporaneous, factual, internally consistent, and monitor-reconciled documentation is the strongest defense anchor.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_6", "question_id": "course_3_module_6_q18", "question_number": 18, "stem": "A CRNA discovers a factual error in an anesthesia record filed the previous day. The correct approach is to:", "choice_a": "Delete the incorrect entry and rewrite the record", "choice_b": "Enter a clearly labeled, dated, and timed addendum that identifies the author, reason for the correction, and specific factual change while leaving the original entry visible", "choice_c": "Notify no one and hope the error is not noticed", "choice_d": "Ask another provider to sign the correction", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Corrections should be transparent addenda that preserve the original entry and explain the change; overwriting or deleting is spoliation.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_6", "question_id": "course_3_module_6_q19", "question_number": 19, "stem": "A CRNA is served with a lawsuit. The FIRST action should be to:", "choice_a": "Post about the case on a professional social network", "choice_b": "Independently contact the plaintiff to explain what happened", "choice_c": "Immediately notify the professional-liability carrier and the employer's risk-management department, and avoid discussing the case outside privileged channels", "choice_d": "Alter the anesthesia record to add clarifying detail", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Prompt notice to the carrier and risk management preserves coverage; unprotected communication and record alteration create severe harm.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_6", "question_id": "course_3_module_6_q20", "question_number": 20, "stem": "A CRNA gives a MAC anesthetic for outpatient endoscopy to a patient with symptomatic shortness of breath at rest, missing cardiac clearance the referring physician had requested. The patient decompensates. The most likely allegation involves:", "choice_a": "Failure to obtain informed consent for endoscopy", "choice_b": "Failure to bill correctly", "choice_c": "Failure to schedule follow-up", "choice_d": "Failure to recognize elevated cardiopulmonary risk, question the absence of cardiac clearance, and delay or escalate before proceeding \u2014 a preoperative assessment failure", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "The recurring closed-claims pattern is failure to recognize elevated risk and pause or escalate before an elective outpatient case.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_6", "question_id": "course_3_module_6_q21", "question_number": 21, "stem": "During a TIVA case, a CRNA discovers 35 minutes into surgery that the propofol infusion line was never connected to the patient's IV. The patient later reports intraoperative awareness. The most important documentation elements are:", "choice_a": "Recognition, corrective action, communication with the anesthesiologist, patient disclosure, and postoperative follow-up", "choice_b": "Only the time surgery ended", "choice_c": "The billing sheet only", "choice_d": "Copy of the surgeon's operative note only", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Documentation should reflect the sequence of recognition, correction, communication, and disclosure/follow-up.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_6", "question_id": "course_3_module_6_q22", "question_number": 22, "stem": "A CRNA in an obstetric hemorrhage case documents epinephrine doses in micrograms instead of milligrams and adds a later addendum changing recorded heart rates from the 200s to the 130s. Defense experts support the clinical care. The predominant risk to the defense is:", "choice_a": "That clinical care was unreasonable", "choice_b": "That documentation is internally inconsistent, opening the CRNA and record to impeachment and undermining an otherwise defensible case", "choice_c": "That the case must automatically be settled", "choice_d": "That AANA standards prohibit epinephrine in obstetrics", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Internal inconsistency and unlabeled late addenda are among the most reliable drivers of settlement in otherwise defensible cases.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_6", "question_id": "course_3_module_6_q23", "question_number": 23, "stem": "Which structure most reliably protects a CRNA who changes jobs frequently and wants long-term coverage for past clinical care?", "choice_a": "Uninsured self-payment plan", "choice_b": "Employer-only coverage after termination", "choice_c": "Occurrence coverage OR claims-made coverage plus committed tail (or nose coverage from a new carrier)", "choice_d": "Claims-made only, without tail", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Occurrence coverage \u2014 or claims-made plus tail/nose \u2014 is the durable structure for CRNAs with employment changes.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_6", "question_id": "course_3_module_6_q24", "question_number": 24, "stem": "A CRNA is under state board of nursing investigation after an adverse event. Civil malpractice defense counsel:", "choice_a": "Is legally the same as license-defense counsel in every jurisdiction", "choice_b": "Cannot be retained by the CRNA", "choice_c": "Automatically also represents the CRNA in board proceedings", "choice_d": "Is legally distinct from license-defense counsel; separate license-defense coverage or counsel is generally needed for board proceedings, which are administrative rather than civil", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Civil litigation and board proceedings are distinct; license-defense coverage funds separate counsel for board matters.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_6", "question_id": "course_3_module_6_q25", "question_number": 25, "stem": "Which of the following best summarizes contemporary trends in CRNA malpractice indemnity payments (2023\u20132026)?", "choice_a": "Severe injury and death continue to drive the majority of high-dollar payments; airway and respiratory events remain leading sources of catastrophic settlements; and CRNA payment patterns are broadly comparable to physician anesthesiology payments when adjusted for practice volume", "choice_b": "Total anesthesia claim volume has increased more than tenfold in the last three years", "choice_c": "Indemnity payments have fallen essentially to zero across all anesthesia specialties", "choice_d": "Median CRNA payments are much lower than physician anesthesiology payments in every jurisdiction", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Recent closed-claims and NPDB data show severity-driven high-dollar payments, airway/respiratory dominance in catastrophic cases, and comparable CRNA/physician payment patterns after adjustment.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}], "course_3_module_7": [{"module_id": "course_3_module_7", "question_id": "course_3_module_7_q01", "question_number": 1, "stem": "In airway malpractice litigation, standard of care is best defined as:", "choice_a": "The level of care, skill, prudence, and judgment that a reasonably competent anesthesia professional would exercise under similar circumstances", "choice_b": "Whatever the individual CRNA believes is appropriate", "choice_c": "The absence of any complication", "choice_d": "Strict adherence to a single national protocol", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Standard of care is not perfection; it is what a reasonably competent peer would do under similar circumstances. Airway cases evaluate foreseeability, alternatives, plan adaptation, and timeliness of rescue.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_7", "question_id": "course_3_module_7_q02", "question_number": 2, "stem": "Which of the following is the most consistent recurring finding in closed-claims analyses of catastrophic airway cases?", "choice_a": "Failure to obtain a chest x-ray prior to induction", "choice_b": "Delayed transition to emergency front-of-neck access (eFONA) during cannot intubate, cannot oxygenate (CICO) events", "choice_c": "Failure to use a specific brand of endotracheal tube", "choice_d": "Failure to use total intravenous anesthesia", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Joffe et al. (2019) and the broader ASA Closed Claims Project consistently identify delayed surgical airway during CICO as the most reliable finding in catastrophic airway claims.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_7", "question_id": "course_3_module_7_q03", "question_number": 3, "stem": "Per the 2022 ASA Practice Guidelines for Management of the Difficult Airway, which of the following is emphasized as a core principle?", "choice_a": "Perform at least five direct laryngoscopy attempts before changing device", "choice_b": "Use only direct laryngoscopy as the primary technique", "choice_c": "Develop a strategy before induction, consider awake intubation when risk factors exist, maintain oxygenation, limit attempts, use cognitive aids, and ensure invasive airway rescue readiness", "choice_d": "Avoid supraglottic airways in all cases of difficult intubation", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "The 2022 ASA guidelines emphasize strategy, awake consideration, oxygenation, limited attempts, cognitive aids, and eFONA readiness (Apfelbaum et al., 2022).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_7", "question_id": "course_3_module_7_q04", "question_number": 4, "stem": "Within the Difficult Airway Society (DAS) framework for the unanticipated difficult airway, 'Plan D' refers to:", "choice_a": "Placing a second supraglottic airway", "choice_b": "Waking the patient and rescheduling under regional anesthesia", "choice_c": "Awake fiberoptic intubation as a rescue technique", "choice_d": "Emergency front-of-neck access (eFONA) \u2014 cannot intubate, cannot oxygenate rescue via scalpel\u2013bougie\u2013tube cricothyrotomy", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "DAS Plan A = mask/intubation; Plan B = SGA; Plan C = mask ventilation and wake; Plan D = eFONA (scalpel\u2013bougie\u2013tube cricothyrotomy) for CICO.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_7", "question_id": "course_3_module_7_q05", "question_number": 5, "stem": "A 58-year-old obese man with obstructive sleep apnea and a documented prior difficult intubation presents for elective cholecystectomy. A video laryngoscope is available in the OR. Which of the following most closely reflects the current (2024\u20132026) standard-of-care approach?", "choice_a": "Use video laryngoscopy as the first-choice device given identified risk factors and immediate availability, and document the rationale", "choice_b": "Rely solely on a supraglottic airway", "choice_c": "Cancel the case and refer to an anesthesiologist", "choice_d": "Attempt direct laryngoscopy first because it is the most familiar technique", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Contemporary practice (2022 ASA guidelines; 2023 DEVICE trial; RCoA 2023) endorses VL as first-choice for anticipated difficulty when available. Documentation of the rationale is protective.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_7", "question_id": "course_3_module_7_q06", "question_number": 6, "stem": "Which of the following preoperative airway documentation entries provides the greatest medico-legal protection for the CRNA?", "choice_a": "'Patient tolerated exam well'", "choice_b": "'Airway: Mallampati III, mouth opening 3 cm, thyromental distance 6 cm, obese (BMI 42) with OSA (STOP-Bang 6). Primary plan: VL with hyperangulated blade; backup: SGA and bougie; awake VL discussed with patient \u2014 declined given adequate anatomy; difficult airway cart at bedside.'", "choice_c": "'Airway WNL'", "choice_d": "'Airway checked'", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Protective airway documentation is specific, includes risk factors, states primary and backup plans, and documents consideration and rationale for awake management when risk factors are present.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_7", "question_id": "course_3_module_7_q07", "question_number": 7, "stem": "During a scheduled colonoscopy under MAC sedation, a morbidly obese patient with untreated OSA becomes cyanotic and unresponsive. The suite does not have capnography connected. Which of the following was the most significant departure from the standard of care?", "choice_a": "Providing MAC rather than general anesthesia", "choice_b": "Use of propofol for sedation", "choice_c": "Absence of continuous waveform capnography during moderate/deep sedation, which is required by the 2020 ASA Standards for Basic Anesthetic Monitoring", "choice_d": "Positioning the patient in the lateral decubitus position", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Since 2011 (with reaffirmation through 2020), ASA Standards for Basic Anesthetic Monitoring require continuous waveform capnography during moderate and deep sedation. Its absence is a clear departure.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_7", "question_id": "course_3_module_7_q08", "question_number": 8, "stem": "Which of the following is the preferred adult emergency front-of-neck access (eFONA) technique per the DAS and 2022 ASA guidelines?", "choice_a": "Retrograde intubation", "choice_b": "Needle cricothyrotomy with jet ventilation", "choice_c": "Percutaneous dilatational tracheostomy", "choice_d": "Scalpel\u2013bougie\u2013tube cricothyrotomy through the cricothyroid membrane", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Scalpel\u2013bougie\u2013tube cricothyrotomy is the preferred adult eFONA technique per DAS 2015 and 2022 ASA guidelines. Needle cricothyrotomy has fallen out of favor in adults.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_7", "question_id": "course_3_module_7_q09", "question_number": 9, "stem": "Awake fiberoptic intubation (AFOI) is most strongly indicated in which of the following scenarios?", "choice_a": "A patient with prior head-and-neck radiation, limited mouth opening, and progressive dysphagia scheduled for tumor debulking", "choice_b": "A patient with a well-controlled asthma exacerbation", "choice_c": "A healthy 25-year-old undergoing appendectomy", "choice_d": "An otherwise healthy patient with a BMI of 26", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "AFOI is indicated for anticipated difficult intubation when spontaneous ventilation preservation is essential \u2014 classic example is post-radiation head-and-neck cancer with airway distortion.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_7", "question_id": "course_3_module_7_q10", "question_number": 10, "stem": "Which of the following is a legitimate indication for awake tracheostomy rather than awake fiberoptic intubation?", "choice_a": "Anxiety about awake procedures", "choice_b": "A large supraglottic tumor causing near-complete airway obstruction in which AFOI is unlikely to succeed and hostile anatomy contra-indicates asleep induction", "choice_c": "Uncomplicated obesity (BMI 32)", "choice_d": "Mild obstructive sleep apnea", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Awake tracheostomy is reserved for patients in whom AFOI is unlikely to succeed \u2014 large supraglottic tumors, near-complete obstruction, or hostile anatomy.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_7", "question_id": "course_3_module_7_q11", "question_number": 11, "stem": "Dental damage claims following anesthesia are best minimized by which combination of practices?", "choice_a": "Skipping the dental exam to save time", "choice_b": "Avoiding intubation in all patients with crowns or bridges", "choice_c": "Preoperative dental documentation, dental guard when indicated, gentle laryngoscopy technique, video laryngoscopy in poor-view patients, and prompt disclosure with referral if injury occurs", "choice_d": "Using only nasal intubation", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Prevention is the combination of preoperative documentation, protective devices when indicated, gentle technique, VL for difficult views, and disclosure/referral if injury occurs.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_7", "question_id": "course_3_module_7_q12", "question_number": 12, "stem": "A patient undergoing prolonged prone spine surgery received large-volume fluid resuscitation. Prior to extubation, which of the following is most appropriate?", "choice_a": "Immediate extubation to shorten OR time", "choice_b": "Deep extubation to reduce cough", "choice_c": "Extubation in the PACU without further assessment", "choice_d": "Structured DAS-based at-risk extubation plan: confirm reversal with TOFR \u2265 0.9, perform cuff-leak test, ensure reintubation equipment and personnel present, and consider airway-exchange catheter", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Prone position and large fluid resuscitation predict airway edema. DAS 2024 extubation guidelines recommend a structured at-risk plan including cuff-leak testing and reintubation readiness.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_7", "question_id": "course_3_module_7_q13", "question_number": 13, "stem": "Which of the following best describes the 'physiologically difficult airway'?", "choice_a": "A patient with normal airway anatomy in whom shock, hypoxemia, metabolic acidosis, pulmonary hypertension, or right-heart failure makes induction and positive-pressure ventilation dangerous", "choice_b": "A patient with a history of asthma", "choice_c": "An airway with abnormal anatomy", "choice_d": "An airway that requires video laryngoscopy", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "The physiologically difficult airway refers to anatomically normal patients whose physiology creates peri-intubation risk \u2014 shock, severe hypoxemia, acidosis, pulmonary hypertension, right-heart failure.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_7", "question_id": "course_3_module_7_q14", "question_number": 14, "stem": "Which action is most consistent with avoiding 'fixation error' during a difficult airway?", "choice_a": "Repositioning the patient repeatedly without changing device", "choice_b": "Limiting repeated attempts with the same operator/device/technique, explicitly calling for help, transitioning to alternate devices (SGA, VL), and declaring CICO when oxygenation cannot be maintained", "choice_c": "Continuing direct laryngoscopy attempts until intubation is successful", "choice_d": "Adding more anesthetic to relax the patient's airway", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Fixation error \u2014 repeated attempts without a change of operator, device, or technique \u2014 is a recurring closed-claims theme. Cognitive aids and structured escalation prevent it.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_7", "question_id": "course_3_module_7_q15", "question_number": 15, "stem": "Under the 2023 ASA Practice Guidelines for monitoring and antagonism of neuromuscular blockade, which train-of-four ratio threshold should be met prior to extubation?", "choice_a": "\u2265 0.4", "choice_b": "\u2265 0.7", "choice_c": "\u2265 0.9", "choice_d": "Extubation may proceed without TOFR measurement", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "The 2023 ASA guidelines recommend quantitative neuromuscular monitoring and a TOFR \u2265 0.9 prior to extubation to avoid residual neuromuscular blockade.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_7", "question_id": "course_3_module_7_q16", "question_number": 16, "stem": "Which of the following documentation errors is most likely to shift a malpractice case decisively against the CRNA?", "choice_a": "Documenting time-stamped drug administration", "choice_b": "Documenting waveform capnography confirmation of tube placement", "choice_c": "Using standard airway-assessment shorthand", "choice_d": "Making a post-event chart entry that is not clearly marked as an addendum and is inconsistent with monitor-recorded vital signs", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Late or altered entries after an adverse event \u2014 particularly when inconsistent with monitor data \u2014 may constitute spoliation of evidence and destroy the CRNA's credibility.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_7", "question_id": "course_3_module_7_q17", "question_number": 17, "stem": "Which of the following patient-related preoperative factors are included in the Kheterpal predictors of difficult mask ventilation?", "choice_a": "Age > 55, BMI > 30, beard, edentulism, snoring history, Mallampati III\u2013IV, limited jaw protrusion, and neck radiation", "choice_b": "Serum sodium and potassium", "choice_c": "Blood type and Rh factor", "choice_d": "Preoperative hemoglobin only", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Kheterpal et al. (2006, 2013) identified age > 55, BMI > 30, beard, edentulism, snoring, Mallampati III\u2013IV, limited jaw protrusion, and neck radiation as difficult-mask-ventilation predictors.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_7", "question_id": "course_3_module_7_q18", "question_number": 18, "stem": "A CRNA is providing MAC for interventional radiology and the proceduralist requests continued sedation despite the patient showing airway compromise. The most appropriate action is:", "choice_a": "Give reversal only if the patient is fully awake", "choice_b": "Pause or terminate the procedure to secure the airway and stabilize the patient \u2014 the CRNA retains authority and obligation to interrupt for airway or physiologic compromise regardless of procedural pressure", "choice_c": "Ask the proceduralist to make the decision", "choice_d": "Continue sedation to maintain schedule", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "The anesthesia provider \u2014 CRNA or physician \u2014 retains authority and obligation to pause or terminate a procedure for airway or physiologic compromise. This should be pre-briefed with the procedural team.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_7", "question_id": "course_3_module_7_q19", "question_number": 19, "stem": "Which cognitive aid is built around three 'lifelines' (face mask, supraglottic airway, tracheal tube) and a central 'CICO Zone' that mandates eFONA?", "choice_a": "The DAS Plan A\u2013D framework", "choice_b": "The ASA Difficult Airway Algorithm", "choice_c": "The Vortex Approach", "choice_d": "The NAP4 report", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "The Vortex Approach (Chrimes, 2016; updated 2024) uses three 'lifelines' and a central CICO zone as a real-time team cognitive aid; it is complementary to the ASA and DAS algorithms.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_7", "question_id": "course_3_module_7_q20", "question_number": 20, "stem": "A patient with anticipated difficult airway based on prior documented difficult intubation is scheduled for elective surgery. The CRNA does not mention awake intubation in the preoperative note or discuss it with the patient. Induction is performed and multiple attempts result in hypoxic injury. The strongest medico-legal criticism will most likely focus on:", "choice_a": "Choice of muscle relaxant", "choice_b": "Room temperature during induction", "choice_c": "Choice of induction drug", "choice_d": "Failure to document consideration of, and rationale for or against, awake tracheal intubation in a patient with clear predictors \u2014 a departure from the 2020 DAS awake tracheal intubation guidelines and the 2022 ASA difficult airway guidelines", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Failure to consider and document awake management in a patient with clear predictors is a common closed-claims criticism and a departure from current DAS/ASA guidelines.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_7", "question_id": "course_3_module_7_q21", "question_number": 21, "stem": "After a difficult airway event, which of the following is the most protective post-event action for both the patient and the CRNA?", "choice_a": "Enter a persistent difficult airway alert into the EHR, provide the patient with a written record of the event (e.g., MedicAlert or DAS Airway Alert card), communicate to primary care, and file an institutional event report per policy", "choice_b": "Delete the difficult airway alert to avoid alarming future providers", "choice_c": "Wait until the next admission to decide whether to document", "choice_d": "Only tell the anesthesiology department verbally", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Persistent EHR airway alerts, written patient records, primary care communication, and institutional reporting protect future patient safety and demonstrate a defensible post-event process.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_7", "question_id": "course_3_module_7_q22", "question_number": 22, "stem": "Which of the following is a growing 2023\u20132025 concern in preoperative airway/aspiration risk assessment that CRNAs should document?", "choice_a": "Use of over-the-counter multivitamins", "choice_b": "Use of glucagon-like peptide-1 (GLP-1) receptor agonists (e.g., semaglutide, tirzepatide), which delay gastric emptying and per ASA 2023 consensus-based guidance affect aspiration risk and fasting recommendations", "choice_c": "Use of a home nebulizer for asthma", "choice_d": "Recent influenza vaccination", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "ASA 2023 consensus-based guidance addresses preoperative management of GLP-1 receptor agonists due to delayed gastric emptying and aspiration concerns. CRNAs should screen and document.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_7", "question_id": "course_3_module_7_q23", "question_number": 23, "stem": "Regarding 'guidelines' such as the 2022 ASA and 2015 DAS airway algorithms, which of the following is most accurate?", "choice_a": "They apply only to anesthesiologists, not CRNAs", "choice_b": "They are legally binding federal regulations", "choice_c": "They are typically described as recommendations rather than binding legal rules, but they strongly influence expert testimony and institutional policy; substantial deviation without documented justification is difficult to defend", "choice_d": "They have no medico-legal relevance", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Guidelines are recommendations, not federal regulations, but they shape expert testimony and institutional standards. Undocumented substantial deviation is a common malpractice vulnerability.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_7", "question_id": "course_3_module_7_q24", "question_number": 24, "stem": "Which of the following best characterizes the medico-legal significance of 'closed-claims' data such as the ASA Closed Claims Project?", "choice_a": "They provide the true incidence of adverse events in anesthesia", "choice_b": "They are legally binding case law", "choice_c": "They are used to license CRNAs", "choice_d": "They are not incidence studies but reveal recurring themes in resolved claims \u2014 enriched for severe outcomes \u2014 that identify preventable patterns and shape risk-management practice", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Closed-claims data are not incidence studies; they reveal recurring, enriched patterns of severe outcomes that inform risk management and expert testimony.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_7", "question_id": "course_3_module_7_q25", "question_number": 25, "stem": "During an unanticipated difficult intubation, SpO\u2082 falls to 60% despite three intubation attempts and two supraglottic airway attempts with inadequate ventilation. The next most appropriate action is to:", "choice_a": "Declare cannot intubate, cannot oxygenate (CICO), continue oxygenation attempts, and immediately move to scalpel\u2013bougie\u2013tube cricothyrotomy", "choice_b": "Attempt one more direct laryngoscopy with a different blade", "choice_c": "Wake the patient", "choice_d": "Give additional muscle relaxant and try again", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Failure of mask, SGA, and intubation with falling SpO\u2082 is the definition of CICO. The 2022 ASA and DAS guidelines mandate declaration and eFONA (scalpel\u2013bougie\u2013tube cricothyrotomy) without further delay.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}], "course_3_module_8": [{"module_id": "course_3_module_8", "question_id": "course_3_module_8_q01", "question_number": 1, "stem": "According to the CDC, the U.S. maternal mortality rate in 2022 was approximately:", "choice_a": "22.3 deaths per 100,000 live births", "choice_b": "132 deaths per 100,000 live births", "choice_c": "5.4 deaths per 100,000 live births", "choice_d": "60.1 deaths per 100,000 live births", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "The CDC/NCHS reported a U.S. maternal mortality rate of 22.3 per 100,000 live births in 2022 \u2014 down from the pandemic-era peak of 32.9 in 2021 but still substantially higher than most peer nations, with a persistent 2.6\u00d7 disparity for Black non-Hispanic mothers.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_8", "question_id": "course_3_module_8_q02", "question_number": 2, "stem": "Per the SOAP/AHA consensus and the 2023 AHA focused update, perimortem cesarean delivery during maternal cardiac arrest should ideally be:", "choice_a": "Deferred until fetal viability is confirmed by ultrasound", "choice_b": "Decided at 4 minutes of arrest with delivery achieved by 5 minutes if there is no return of spontaneous circulation", "choice_c": "Performed only if the fetus is \u2265 34 weeks", "choice_d": "Delayed until the anesthesiologist arrives", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "SOAP (Lipman et al., 2014) and the 2023 AHA guidelines recommend perimortem cesarean decision at 4 minutes and delivery by 5 minutes if no ROSC \u2014 regardless of fetal viability \u2014 to relieve aortocaval compression and improve maternal survival.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_8", "question_id": "course_3_module_8_q03", "question_number": 3, "stem": "During maternal cardiac arrest, current SOAP/AHA guidance recommends:", "choice_a": "Left lateral tilt of the table at 30\u00b0", "choice_b": "Reduced epinephrine dosing to protect the fetus", "choice_c": "Manual left uterine displacement with the patient supine on a firm surface, IV access above the diaphragm, and standard ACLS drug doses", "choice_d": "Avoiding defibrillation until after delivery", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Table tilt degrades chest compression quality. AHA 2023 and SOAP recommend manual LUD, supine positioning, IV access above the diaphragm (to avoid aortocaval interference), standard ACLS drug doses, and standard defibrillation energy.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_8", "question_id": "course_3_module_8_q04", "question_number": 4, "stem": "A 32-year-old parturient develops sudden hypoxemia, hypotension, seizure, and DIC 5 minutes after spontaneous vaginal delivery. Which diagnosis best fits the SMFM/AFE Foundation clinical criteria for amniotic fluid embolism?", "choice_a": "Neuraxial-related hypotension", "choice_b": "Any cardiovascular collapse during labor", "choice_c": "Isolated postpartum hemorrhage with hypotension", "choice_d": "Sudden cardiorespiratory arrest or hypotension with respiratory compromise, followed by overt DIC, occurring during labor or within 30 minutes of placental delivery, with no fever \u2265 38 \u00b0C", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Clark et al. (2016), reaffirmed in the 2023 SMFM consult, define AFE by all four criteria: cardiorespiratory arrest or hypotension with respiratory compromise, overt DIC after the initial signs, onset during labor or within 30 minutes of placental delivery, and no fever \u2265 38 \u00b0C during labor.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_8", "question_id": "course_3_module_8_q05", "question_number": 5, "stem": "Under the 2021 SOAP interdisciplinary consensus on neuraxial procedures in obstetric patients with thrombocytopenia, neuraxial anesthesia is generally considered reasonable when the platelet count is:", "choice_a": "\u2265 70 \u00d7 10\u2079/L, stable, with no additional bleeding risk factors", "choice_b": "\u2265 150 \u00d7 10\u2079/L", "choice_c": "\u2265 50 \u00d7 10\u2079/L with no additional risk factors", "choice_d": "\u2265 20 \u00d7 10\u2079/L", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "The 2021 SOAP consensus (Bauer et al.) considers neuraxial reasonable at a stable platelet count \u2265 70 \u00d7 10\u2079/L with no additional risk factors; 50\u201370 \u00d7 10\u2079/L requires individualization; < 50 generally favors alternatives.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_8", "question_id": "course_3_module_8_q06", "question_number": 6, "stem": "A laboring patient develops sudden numbness ascending to the shoulders, dyspnea, dysphonia, and BP 68/32 five minutes after an epidural top-up. The MOST appropriate immediate CRNA action is to:", "choice_a": "Administer additional epidural anesthetic to \u201cbreak through\u201d the block", "choice_b": "Call for help; support ventilation and consider intubation; administer phenylephrine/ephedrine, IV fluid, and left uterine displacement; notify OB and neonatal teams", "choice_c": "Continue observation because these symptoms often resolve", "choice_d": "Reverse the epidural with intralipid immediately without other supportive care", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Findings are classic for high or total spinal anesthesia. Immediate response is airway support, vasopressor and fluid resuscitation, LUD, and OB/neonatal notification. Lipid is not the primary treatment for high spinal (it is for LAST).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_8", "question_id": "course_3_module_8_q07", "question_number": 7, "stem": "Which of the following is the FIRST-LINE pharmacologic treatment for local anesthetic systemic toxicity (LAST) per ASRA 2020 (updated 2023) guidance?", "choice_a": "Vasopressin", "choice_b": "Standard 1 mg IV epinephrine bolus", "choice_c": "20% lipid emulsion 1.5 mL/kg lean body weight bolus followed by 0.25 mL/kg/min infusion, with epinephrine limited to \u2264 1 mcg/kg boluses", "choice_d": "Calcium channel blockers", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "ASRA recommends immediate 20% lipid emulsion 1.5 mL/kg bolus followed by 0.25 mL/kg/min, with epinephrine dosing reduced to \u2264 1 mcg/kg boluses and avoidance of vasopressin, calcium channel blockers, and beta-blockers.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_8", "question_id": "course_3_module_8_q08", "question_number": 8, "stem": "A parturient with a labor epidural that has required frequent boluses for a unilateral block now needs a Category 1 (crash) cesarean for fetal bradycardia. The MOST liability-defensible anesthetic plan is generally to:", "choice_a": "Perform a spinal on top of the existing epidural without any dose reduction", "choice_b": "Delay surgery to place a new epidural", "choice_c": "Rebolus the epidural and hope for adequate conversion", "choice_d": "Proceed with general anesthesia via rapid-sequence induction with video laryngoscopy, cricoid per policy, and difficult-airway backup immediately available", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "A poorly functioning labor epidural is unlikely to convert reliably. For a true crash cesarean, general anesthesia with difficult-airway readiness is safer than repeated attempts to salvage an unreliable block, and delay is not acceptable in a Category 1 setting.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_8", "question_id": "course_3_module_8_q09", "question_number": 9, "stem": "Per ACOG Practice Bulletin No. 205 (reaffirmed), a trial of labor after cesarean (TOLAC) should be offered ONLY in facilities that have:", "choice_a": "Immediate availability of anesthesia personnel and the ability to begin cesarean delivery expeditiously if uterine rupture occurs", "choice_b": "A dedicated obstetric ICU", "choice_c": "Board-certified anesthesiologists on call from home", "choice_d": "Only CRNAs credentialed in cardiac anesthesia", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "ACOG PB 205 explicitly requires immediate availability of anesthesia and the ability to begin cesarean delivery expeditiously; the guidance is driven by the rapid decision-to-incision requirement in uterine rupture.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_8", "question_id": "course_3_module_8_q10", "question_number": 10, "stem": "A patient with a suspected accidental dural puncture during labor epidural placement develops a bilateral throbbing headache 24 hours postpartum that improves when supine and worsens when upright. Which additional feature would MOST require urgent evaluation for an alternative diagnosis rather than routine PDPH management?", "choice_a": "Onset 24 hours after neuraxial placement", "choice_b": "New focal neurologic deficit, seizure, altered mental status, or a thunderclap or non-positional pattern", "choice_c": "Neck stiffness and mild tinnitus", "choice_d": "Photophobia", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Per the 2023 international PDPH consensus, atypical features \u2014 focal neurologic deficits, seizure, altered mental status, thunderclap or non-positional headache \u2014 require urgent evaluation for preeclampsia, ICH, CVST, meningitis, subdural hematoma, or PRES/RCVS.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_8", "question_id": "course_3_module_8_q11", "question_number": 11, "stem": "Per ACOG Practice Bulletin No. 183, postpartum hemorrhage is defined as:", "choice_a": "Blood loss requiring \u2265 4 units RBC only", "choice_b": "Any blood loss > 250 mL", "choice_c": "Cumulative blood loss \u2265 1,000 mL OR blood loss accompanied by signs or symptoms of hypovolemia within 24 hours after birth, regardless of delivery route", "choice_d": "Blood loss > 500 mL after vaginal delivery only", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "ACOG PB 183 defines PPH as cumulative blood loss \u2265 1,000 mL or blood loss with hypovolemia signs within 24 hours regardless of delivery route, unifying the prior separate vaginal and cesarean thresholds.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_8", "question_id": "course_3_module_8_q12", "question_number": 12, "stem": "Per WOMAN Trial and current ACOG/WHO guidance, tranexamic acid (TXA) for postpartum hemorrhage should be administered:", "choice_a": "Only for uterine atony", "choice_b": "Only after 4 units of blood have been transfused", "choice_c": "Only for hemorrhage after cesarean", "choice_d": "1 g IV within 3 hours of hemorrhage onset, with a second 1 g dose if bleeding continues after 30 minutes", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "The WOMAN Trial (2017) showed reduced maternal death from bleeding when TXA 1 g IV was administered within 3 hours of PPH onset; a repeat dose can be given if bleeding continues.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_8", "question_id": "course_3_module_8_q13", "question_number": 13, "stem": "Contemporary obstetric anesthesia guidance identifies which vasopressor as generally FIRST-LINE for treating spinal-induced hypotension at elective cesarean delivery?", "choice_a": "Phenylephrine, often as a prophylactic infusion", "choice_b": "Vasopressin", "choice_c": "Ephedrine", "choice_d": "Norepinephrine 32 mcg/mL bolus dosing", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Contemporary evidence and SOAP/ASA guidance favor phenylephrine as first-line (frequently as a prophylactic infusion) because it preserves uteroplacental perfusion better than ephedrine and reduces neonatal acidosis.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_8", "question_id": "course_3_module_8_q14", "question_number": 14, "stem": "Per the RCOG four-category framework widely adopted in the U.S., a Category 1 cesarean delivery indicates:", "choice_a": "An elective cesarean at term", "choice_b": "Immediate threat to life of mother or fetus; decision-to-incision goal is typically \u2264 15\u201330 minutes", "choice_c": "Delivery needed within a few days but not urgent", "choice_d": "Maternal request without medical indication", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Category 1 is immediate threat to life of mother or fetus, with decision-to-incision typically \u2264 15\u201330 minutes; Category 2 involves compromise but not immediately life-threatening; Category 3 requires early delivery without compromise; Category 4 is elective.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_8", "question_id": "course_3_module_8_q15", "question_number": 15, "stem": "In the AANA Foundation obstetric closed-claims review (Clayton et al., 2018), the leading identified cause of MATERNAL death was:", "choice_a": "Failed intubation", "choice_b": "High spinal", "choice_c": "Hemorrhage", "choice_d": "PDPH", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Clayton et al. (2018) identified hemorrhage as the leading cause of maternal death in AANA Foundation obstetric closed claims, with recurring themes of care delay, communication failure, and incomplete documentation.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_8", "question_id": "course_3_module_8_q16", "question_number": 16, "stem": "In the Kovacheva et al. (2019) contemporary medicolegal review of 106 obstetric anesthesia claims (2005\u20132015), the SINGLE LARGEST claim category was:", "choice_a": "PDPH", "choice_b": "Maternal death", "choice_c": "Newborn death", "choice_d": "Maternal nerve injury", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Kovacheva et al. (2019) found maternal nerve injury was the largest single claim category, while maternal death or brain injury claims were more likely to result in payment.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_8", "question_id": "course_3_module_8_q17", "question_number": 17, "stem": "A patient with severe preeclampsia has BP 172/112 that has persisted for 20 minutes. Per current ACOG/AIM guidance, IV antihypertensive therapy should generally be initiated within:", "choice_a": "30\u201360 minutes", "choice_b": "5 minutes", "choice_c": "6 hours", "choice_d": "24 hours", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Persistent severe-range hypertension (SBP \u2265 160 or DBP \u2265 110 for \u2265 15 minutes) is an obstetric emergency; ACOG/AIM safety bundles require initiation of IV antihypertensive (labetalol, hydralazine, or nifedipine per protocol) within 30\u201360 minutes.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_8", "question_id": "course_3_module_8_q18", "question_number": 18, "stem": "During emergency cesarean delivery under general anesthesia, which strategy BEST reduces the risk of intraoperative awareness in the obstetric patient?", "choice_a": "Avoid volatile anesthetics entirely", "choice_b": "Use adequate induction dosing, transition rapidly to volatile anesthetic \u2265 0.5 MAC after intubation, and consider low-dose midazolam after delivery", "choice_c": "Rely only on high-dose opioid", "choice_d": "Underdose induction agents to minimize neonatal drug exposure", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Underdosing induction agents \u2014 out of concern for neonatal effects \u2014 is a leading cause of awareness. Adequate induction, rapid transition to volatile anesthetic \u2265 0.5 MAC, and consideration of midazolam post-delivery are recommended by NAP5 obstetric findings.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_8", "question_id": "course_3_module_8_q19", "question_number": 19, "stem": "During cesarean delivery under neuraxial anesthesia, the patient reports sharp intolerable pain at incision. The MOST liability-defensible next step is to:", "choice_a": "Ask the family to reassure the patient", "choice_b": "Give an oral sedative", "choice_c": "Immediately acknowledge the complaint, notify the surgeon, assess the block, treat pain (supplemental analgesia), consider conversion to general anesthesia when appropriate, and document the assessment, treatment, and communication", "choice_d": "Reassure the patient and continue surgery to expedite delivery", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Kovacheva et al. and other closed-claims data identify dismissing patient-reported pain as a major liability driver. Immediate acknowledgment, assessment, treatment, and, when appropriate, conversion to GA are required.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_8", "question_id": "course_3_module_8_q20", "question_number": 20, "stem": "Which of the following BEST reflects the CRNA\u2019s appropriate role in fetal heart-rate tracing documentation on the anesthesia record?", "choice_a": "The CRNA should ignore FHR data", "choice_b": "The CRNA should provide the primary FHR interpretation", "choice_c": "The CRNA should copy the FHR tracing image into the anesthesia record", "choice_d": "The CRNA should document what was communicated by nursing or OB, relevant maternal physiologic changes, interventions performed, and team response \u2014 without making definitive obstetric FHR interpretations unless credentialed to do so", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Unless credentialed for FHR interpretation under facility policy, the CRNA should record what was communicated by OB/nursing, what maternal physiology was observed, and what anesthesia interventions were performed \u2014 not independent FHR interpretations.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_8", "question_id": "course_3_module_8_q21", "question_number": 21, "stem": "Per ACOG Levels of Maternal Care and most facility policies, a Level III or IV birthing center should have:", "choice_a": "In-house anesthesia coverage 24 hours a day, 7 days a week", "choice_b": "Anesthesia only for cesarean deliveries", "choice_c": "Anesthesia on call from home", "choice_d": "Anesthesia available within 60 minutes", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "ACOG/SMFM Levels of Maternal Care (Obstetric Care Consensus No. 9) expects Level III/IV facilities to have in-house anesthesia 24/7 because these centers manage the highest-acuity maternal cases.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_8", "question_id": "course_3_module_8_q22", "question_number": 22, "stem": "Which practice BEST supports culturally responsive, legally defensible informed consent for obstetric anesthesia in a patient with limited English proficiency?", "choice_a": "Have the patient sign a translated form without discussion", "choice_b": "Use a qualified medical interpreter (in-person, phone, or video), confirm understanding with teach-back, and document the interpreter name/vendor and language", "choice_c": "Ask the patient\u2019s spouse to translate consent", "choice_d": "Use gestures and simplified English only", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Family members should not translate complex anesthesia consent \u2014 they may omit, soften, or alter information. Qualified interpreters, teach-back, and documentation of interpreter use are required and are also mandated under Section 1557 of the ACA.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_8", "question_id": "course_3_module_8_q23", "question_number": 23, "stem": "A patient with suspected placenta accreta spectrum is scheduled for cesarean hysterectomy. Which anesthesia plan is MOST consistent with contemporary SMFM/SOAP guidance?", "choice_a": "Neuraxial anesthesia is contraindicated in all PAS cases", "choice_b": "Routine spinal only, no additional preparation", "choice_c": "Delivery at a facility with 24-hour blood bank and MTP, two large-bore IVs plus arterial line, cell salvage availability, blood-product pre-positioning, multidisciplinary team, and planned ICU postoperative care \u2014 with a low threshold to convert to GA if hemorrhage becomes severe", "choice_d": "General anesthesia is always required", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Contemporary SMFM/SOAP guidance supports management at a PAS-experienced Level III/IV center with pre-positioned blood products, invasive monitoring, cell salvage, multidisciplinary team readiness, and planned ICU care. Neuraxial with low threshold to convert to GA is often appropriate.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_8", "question_id": "course_3_module_8_q24", "question_number": 24, "stem": "A CRNA discovers that a labor epidural placed 2 hours ago has provided only unilateral analgesia and required repeated boluses. The patient may need cesarean delivery for arrest of labor. The MOST proactive risk-management action is to:", "choice_a": "Document the block as \u201cadequate\u201d to avoid a chart flag", "choice_b": "Wait until the surgical decision is made before troubleshooting", "choice_c": "Continue the epidural and hope it works if needed", "choice_d": "Reassess the catheter now, troubleshoot position, and replace proactively if the block remains inadequate \u2014 rather than relying on it in an emergency", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Closed-claims data (ASA, AANA Foundation, Kovacheva) all identify failed epidural conversion as a major preventable risk. Proactive troubleshooting or replacement before an emergency is far safer than reactive management under crash conditions.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_8", "question_id": "course_3_module_8_q25", "question_number": 25, "stem": "Which of the following is the MOST accurate summary of the unifying message from ASA, AANA Foundation, and Kovacheva 2019 closed-claims data on obstetric anesthesia?", "choice_a": "Most obstetric anesthesia liability arises from system failures \u2014 delay, communication, documentation, and failure to convert a warning into escalation \u2014 rather than knowledge failure", "choice_b": "Most obstetric anesthesia liability is driven by patient refusal of care", "choice_c": "Most obstetric anesthesia liability is unrelated to CRNA practice", "choice_d": "Most obstetric anesthesia liability arises from lack of clinical knowledge", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Across databases, the dominant preventable themes are delay, communication failure, incomplete documentation, and failure to escalate \u2014 system issues \u2014 rather than individual knowledge deficits. Anticipation, escalation, closed-loop communication, and contemporaneous documentation are the defensive keys.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}], "course_3_module_9": [{"module_id": "course_3_module_9", "question_id": "course_3_module_9_q01", "question_number": 1, "stem": "Which of the following most accurately describes the three core HIPAA rules that apply to CRNA practice?", "choice_a": "Privacy Rule (uses and disclosures of PHI), Security Rule (safeguards for ePHI), and Breach Notification Rule (response to impermissible use or disclosure of unsecured PHI)", "choice_b": "Federal Rule, State Rule, and Facility Rule", "choice_c": "Meaningful Use Rule, MACRA Rule, and Interoperability Rule", "choice_d": "Consent Rule, Encryption Rule, and Litigation Rule", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "HIPAA's three core rules relevant to CRNAs are the Privacy Rule (45 CFR \u00a7\u00a7164.500\u2013164.534), the Security Rule (\u00a7\u00a7164.302\u2013164.318), and the Breach Notification Rule (\u00a7\u00a7164.400\u2013164.414).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_9", "question_id": "course_3_module_9_q02", "question_number": 2, "stem": "Under the HIPAA Privacy Rule, disclosure of PHI for treatment, payment, and health-care operations (TPO):", "choice_a": "Is only permitted for treatment, never for payment or operations", "choice_b": "Is generally permitted without separate written authorization when the disclosure supports patient care and complies with organizational safeguards (45 CFR \u00a7164.506)", "choice_c": "Requires a signed HIPAA authorization every time", "choice_d": "Is prohibited without a court order", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "45 CFR \u00a7164.506 permits use and disclosure of PHI for TPO without separate written authorization when the disclosure supports patient care and complies with safeguards.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_9", "question_id": "course_3_module_9_q03", "question_number": 3, "stem": "The minimum necessary standard (45 CFR \u00a7164.502(b)) generally does NOT apply to which of the following disclosures?", "choice_a": "A disclosure to a business associate for billing", "choice_b": "A disclosure for a quality improvement project", "choice_c": "A disclosure by one health-care provider to another for treatment", "choice_d": "A disclosure for peer review", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "The minimum necessary standard does not restrict disclosures to or requests by a health-care provider for treatment (nor disclosures to the patient, under valid authorization, to HHS, required by law, or required for compliance).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_9", "question_id": "course_3_module_9_q04", "question_number": 4, "stem": "A CRNA opens the chart of a coworker who was admitted overnight for a minor surgery, out of concern, and does not share the information. Which statement is most accurate?", "choice_a": "This is acceptable because no information was shared", "choice_b": "This is acceptable if the coworker is a friend", "choice_c": "This is only a violation if the coworker complains", "choice_d": "This may still constitute a HIPAA violation \u2014 technical ability to access a chart does not equal authorization, and audit controls will detect the access", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Under 45 CFR \u00a7164.312(b), audit controls track access. Opening a chart without a care-related or approved purpose is unauthorized access even if no information is shared \u2014 a \"no harm intended\" defense is not valid.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_9", "question_id": "course_3_module_9_q05", "question_number": 5, "stem": "Which of the following are required elements of a Business Associate Agreement (BAA) under 45 CFR \u00a7164.504(e)?", "choice_a": "Permitted uses of PHI, safeguards, breach and security-incident reporting, subcontractor flow-down, patient-rights support, HHS access, return/destruction of PHI at termination, and termination authority for material breach", "choice_b": "Only fee-schedule and payment terms", "choice_c": "Only encryption requirements", "choice_d": "Only insurance coverage and indemnification", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "45 CFR \u00a7164.504(e) enumerates the required elements: permitted/required uses, safeguards, breach/incident reporting, subcontractor flow-down, patient-rights support, HHS access, return/destruction at termination, and termination authority for material breach.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_9", "question_id": "course_3_module_9_q06", "question_number": 6, "stem": "The 2024 Change Healthcare cyberattack is significant to CRNA practice primarily because:", "choice_a": "It was limited to dental practices", "choice_b": "It was a business-associate compromise that ultimately affected approximately 190 million individuals \u2014 the largest health-care data breach in U.S. history \u2014 and disrupted anesthesia group billing, prior authorization, and claims for months", "choice_c": "It has no relevance to HIPAA", "choice_d": "It only affected physician offices, not CRNAs", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "The February 2024 Change Healthcare ransomware event affected ~190 million individuals via a business associate and disrupted health-care operations nationwide, illustrating business-associate risk and reinforcing the need for BAAs and cybersecurity controls.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_9", "question_id": "course_3_module_9_q07", "question_number": 7, "stem": "Which of the following was proposed in the December 2024 HIPAA Security Rule Notice of Proposed Rulemaking (NPRM)?", "choice_a": "Repeal of the Breach Notification Rule", "choice_b": "Elimination of the Privacy Rule", "choice_c": "Elimination of the addressable/required distinction; mandatory encryption of ePHI; multi-factor authentication; written risk analyses and asset inventories; incident-response timelines; and annual business-associate written verification", "choice_d": "Removal of patient right-of-access requirements", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "The 2024 NPRM (89 Fed. Reg. 101340) proposes to eliminate addressable specifications, mandate encryption and MFA, require written risk analyses and asset inventories, impose contingency/incident timelines, and require annual written business-associate assurances.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_9", "question_id": "course_3_module_9_q08", "question_number": 8, "stem": "Per the HIPAA Breach Notification Rule, a covered entity must notify affected individuals of a breach of unsecured PHI:", "choice_a": "Within 30 days of the impermissible use or disclosure", "choice_b": "Only at annual reporting", "choice_c": "Only if more than 1,000 individuals are affected", "choice_d": "Without unreasonable delay and no later than 60 calendar days after discovery of the breach", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "45 CFR \u00a7164.404 requires individual notification without unreasonable delay and in no case later than 60 calendar days after discovery of the breach.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_9", "question_id": "course_3_module_9_q09", "question_number": 9, "stem": "For breaches affecting 500 or more individuals in a state or jurisdiction, the covered entity must:", "choice_a": "Notify affected individuals, notify HHS contemporaneously (and in no case later than 60 calendar days after discovery), and notify prominent media outlets serving the state or jurisdiction", "choice_b": "Notify only the HHS Secretary annually", "choice_c": "Notify only the affected individuals", "choice_d": "Notify only the state attorney general", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "For breaches \u2265500 individuals in a state or jurisdiction, 45 CFR \u00a7164.406\u2013164.408 require individual notice, contemporaneous HHS notice (no later than 60 days), and media notice in that state or jurisdiction.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_9", "question_id": "course_3_module_9_q10", "question_number": 10, "stem": "A business associate that discovers a breach of unsecured PHI must notify the covered entity:", "choice_a": "Only at contract renewal", "choice_b": "Without unreasonable delay and no later than 60 calendar days after discovery of the breach at the business associate", "choice_c": "Within 30 calendar days", "choice_d": "Within 7 calendar days", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "45 CFR \u00a7164.410 requires business associates to notify the covered entity without unreasonable delay and no later than 60 calendar days after discovery of the breach.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_9", "question_id": "course_3_module_9_q11", "question_number": 11, "stem": "A CRNA needs to text an on-call anesthesiologist about an add-on case with the patient's medical record number and airway concern. Which channel is HIPAA-compliant?", "choice_a": "Standard SMS from a personal iPhone", "choice_b": "WhatsApp", "choice_c": "A facility-approved secure clinical messaging platform (e.g., TigerConnect, Halo Health, Epic Secure Chat, Vocera) that provides encryption, audit logging, unique authentication, and operates under a BAA", "choice_d": "iMessage between personal phones", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Consumer SMS/iMessage/WhatsApp typically lack HIPAA safeguards and lack a BAA. Only facility-approved secure clinical messaging platforms meet Security Rule transmission-security and audit requirements.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_9", "question_id": "course_3_module_9_q12", "question_number": 12, "stem": "A CRNA takes a photo of an interesting airway with a personal phone during a case, intending to use it later for teaching. Which of the following is the most correct action?", "choice_a": "The photo can be posted to a private social-media group", "choice_b": "The photo is fine because HIPAA does not cover images", "choice_c": "The photo is fine if the patient's face is not visible", "choice_d": "The photo should have been taken with an approved facility image-capture system with any required consent; taking or storing identifiable images on a personal device is not compliant with the Security Rule and organizational policy, even if the face is obscured", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Clinical photography must occur via approved equipment and storage. Wristbands, MRN visibility, monitor detail, or surrounding context can still make an image identifiable. Personal-device storage of identifiable images generally violates policy and HIPAA.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_9", "question_id": "course_3_module_9_q13", "question_number": 13, "stem": "A CRNA posts a de-identified anesthesia \"war story\" on social media describing an unusual airway complication during a high-profile trauma at their hospital. Which statement is most accurate?", "choice_a": "This may still be a HIPAA violation because timing, location, diagnosis, procedure, image details, or community context can make the patient identifiable \u2014 nurse-related discipline for such posts is well-documented", "choice_b": "It's acceptable as long as friends only see the post", "choice_c": "This is fine because no name was used", "choice_d": "Social media is not covered by HIPAA", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "OCR and boards of nursing have consistently found that \"de-identified\" case posts can still identify a patient through community context. Do not post clinical detail without organizational authorization and any required patient authorization.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_9", "question_id": "course_3_module_9_q14", "question_number": 14, "stem": "Under the 2024 inflation-adjusted HITECH civil monetary penalty tiers, which tier applies to violations attributable to willful neglect that are NOT corrected within 30 days?", "choice_a": "Tier 2 (reasonable cause, not willful neglect)", "choice_b": "Tier 4 (willful neglect, not corrected within 30 days) \u2014 with the highest per-violation minimums and maximums, up to the annual cap", "choice_c": "Tier 1 (no knowledge)", "choice_d": "Tier 3 (willful neglect, corrected within 30 days)", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "42 USC \u00a71320d-5 and 45 CFR \u00a7160.404 establish four tiers. Tier 4 (willful neglect, not corrected within 30 days) carries the highest minimum (~$68,928) and maximum per violation, subject to the annual cap.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_9", "question_id": "course_3_module_9_q15", "question_number": 15, "stem": "A patient requests a copy of their anesthesia record. What is the CRNA's most correct action under the Right of Access Rule (45 CFR \u00a7164.524)?", "choice_a": "Print the record and hand it to the patient at bedside", "choice_b": "Refuse because CRNAs cannot release records", "choice_c": "Route the request through the organization's release-of-information process so identity verification, timeline (generally 30 days), format, cost-based fee, and any denial-review procedure are handled correctly", "choice_d": "Email the record from a personal account", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Patients have a right of access, but release requires identity verification, form/format handling, and reasonable fee calculation. Route through release-of-information; informal release bypasses controls and audit trail.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_9", "question_id": "course_3_module_9_q16", "question_number": 16, "stem": "Under 45 CFR \u00a7164.524, generally within how many calendar days must a covered entity provide a patient with requested access to their PHI (with a one-time extension of up to 30 additional days permitted)?", "choice_a": "180 calendar days", "choice_b": "10 calendar days", "choice_c": "60 calendar days", "choice_d": "30 calendar days", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "45 CFR \u00a7164.524 requires access to be provided generally within 30 calendar days, with a one-time 30-day extension permitted if the patient is notified in writing with the reason.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_9", "question_id": "course_3_module_9_q17", "question_number": 17, "stem": "Which of the following best describes OCR's Right of Access Initiative as reflected in 2024\u20132025 enforcement?", "choice_a": "OCR has announced 50+ Right of Access settlements against covered entities that failed to provide timely, reasonably-priced patient access \u2014 including 2024\u20132025 settlements ranging from $15,000 to over $200,000", "choice_b": "OCR settlements have all been over $10 million", "choice_c": "OCR only enforces breach notification, not access rights", "choice_d": "OCR has abandoned right-of-access enforcement", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "OCR has publicly announced 50+ Right of Access settlements, including 2024\u20132025 settlements from $15,000 to over $240,000 for failure to provide timely, reasonable-fee patient access.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_9", "question_id": "course_3_module_9_q18", "question_number": 18, "stem": "Under HIPAA, a successful ransomware attack that encrypts electronic PHI is:", "choice_a": "Only reportable if the ransom is paid", "choice_b": "Presumed to be a reportable breach unless the covered entity demonstrates a low probability that PHI was compromised under the four-factor risk assessment (45 CFR \u00a7164.402)", "choice_c": "Only a breach if patients complain", "choice_d": "Never a breach because the attacker did not view the data", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "OCR guidance treats ransomware infection of ePHI as a presumed breach because attacker encryption is an impermissible acquisition; the covered entity must demonstrate low probability of compromise via the four-factor assessment.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_9", "question_id": "course_3_module_9_q19", "question_number": 19, "stem": "Which of the following is a Security Rule technical safeguard under 45 CFR \u00a7164.312?", "choice_a": "Facility access control (badge access)", "choice_b": "Sanction policies", "choice_c": "Unique user identification, automatic logoff, encryption, audit controls, and transmission security", "choice_d": "Workforce training", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "45 CFR \u00a7164.312 covers technical safeguards \u2014 unique user identification, automatic logoff, encryption/decryption, audit controls, integrity, authentication, and transmission security. Training and sanctions are administrative; badge access is physical.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_9", "question_id": "course_3_module_9_q20", "question_number": 20, "stem": "A CRNA discovers that an unencrypted personal laptop containing a spreadsheet of preop patient identifiers has been stolen from her car. What is the most appropriate initial action?", "choice_a": "Say nothing and try to buy a replacement laptop", "choice_b": "Wait to see if the laptop is returned before reporting anything", "choice_c": "Post about the theft on social media to see if anyone has seen it", "choice_d": "Report immediately through the organization's incident/privacy channel so risk assessment, containment, breach evaluation, and any required notification timeline can begin \u2014 and cooperate with the investigation", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Prompt internal reporting is essential; delays interfere with containment, four-factor breach analysis, notification timelines (60-day maximum), and mitigation. The unencrypted device also creates a Security Rule concern.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_9", "question_id": "course_3_module_9_q21", "question_number": 21, "stem": "Under 45 CFR \u00a7164.522(a)(1)(vi), a covered entity is REQUIRED to agree to a patient's requested restriction on disclosure of PHI to a health plan when:", "choice_a": "The patient has paid in full out of pocket for the relevant service and the disclosure is for payment or health-care operations and is not otherwise required by law", "choice_b": "The health plan waives the requirement", "choice_c": "The service was elective", "choice_d": "The patient asks in writing regardless of payment status", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "45 CFR \u00a7164.522(a)(1)(vi) requires the covered entity to honor the restriction when the patient has paid in full out of pocket and the disclosure to a health plan is for payment or operations and is not otherwise required by law.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_9", "question_id": "course_3_module_9_q22", "question_number": 22, "stem": "A CRNA is asked by a vendor demonstrating a new anesthesia device to email de-identified case examples to the vendor's training team. Which of the following is most correct?", "choice_a": "Send from a personal email account for convenience", "choice_b": "Confirm the vendor is on the approved list with a signed BAA if PHI is involved, ensure any de-identification meets HIPAA Safe-Harbor or Expert-Determination standards (45 CFR \u00a7164.514), and use organization-approved communication channels", "choice_c": "Upload to the vendor's public cloud folder", "choice_d": "Send freely \u2014 de-identification removes HIPAA obligations", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "If PHI is involved, a BAA is required. If truly de-identified per \u00a7164.514 Safe-Harbor (removal of 18 identifiers) or Expert Determination, HIPAA no longer applies \u2014 but the CRNA must ensure the standard is met and use approved channels.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_9", "question_id": "course_3_module_9_q23", "question_number": 23, "stem": "A patient in the PACU objects to disclosure of any of her clinical information to her adult son who has been in the waiting room. What is the CRNA's appropriate response?", "choice_a": "Ignore the patient's objection because HIPAA requires family notification", "choice_b": "Provide the son with a full clinical update because he is family", "choice_c": "Respect the patient's objection; when the patient is present and has decision-making capacity, HIPAA (45 CFR \u00a7164.510(b)) generally permits the patient to agree, object, or restrict disclosure to family or friends", "choice_d": "Ask the son for his authorization", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "45 CFR \u00a7164.510(b) allows the patient with capacity, when present, to agree or object to disclosure to family or friends. The CRNA must respect the objection.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_9", "question_id": "course_3_module_9_q24", "question_number": 24, "stem": "Which of the following is a HIPAA-compliant use of an anesthesia record for education?", "choice_a": "Posting the record on a personal blog with the patient's name removed", "choice_b": "Sharing the record on a public online forum", "choice_c": "Emailing the record to friends outside the organization", "choice_d": "Presenting the case at a facility-sponsored M&M; or educational conference using de-identified information, or using identifiable information only when consistent with organizational policy and any required authorization", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Approved educational use \u2014 with de-identification when possible, or identifiable information only when policy and any required authorization support it \u2014 is permissible. Personal blogs, public forums, and outside emails are not.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_9", "question_id": "course_3_module_9_q25", "question_number": 25, "stem": "Criminal penalties under 42 U.S.C. \u00a71320d-6 for a person who obtains individually identifiable health information under false pretenses can include:", "choice_a": "Up to $100,000 and 5 years imprisonment", "choice_b": "Up to $50,000 and 1 year imprisonment", "choice_c": "Up to $250,000 and 10 years imprisonment", "choice_d": "A written warning only", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "42 USC \u00a71320d-6 imposes up to $50,000/1 year for a knowing violation, up to $100,000/5 years when committed under false pretenses, and up to $250,000/10 years when committed with intent to sell, transfer, or use PHI for personal gain, commercial advantage, or malicious harm.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}], "course_3_module_10": [{"module_id": "course_3_module_10", "question_id": "course_3_module_10_q01", "question_number": 1, "stem": "Which of the following best describes the primary purpose of CRNA expert witness testimony?", "choice_a": "To educate the trier of fact about the nurse anesthesia standard of care and how it applies to the facts of the case", "choice_b": "To advocate for the retaining attorney\u2019s theory of the case", "choice_c": "To recommend a specific settlement value", "choice_d": "To decide whether the defendant is legally liable", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Expert testimony is education, not advocacy. The CRNA expert helps the fact-finder understand the applicable standard of care; the expert does not decide the case or advocate for either side.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_10", "question_id": "course_3_module_10_q02", "question_number": 2, "stem": "Which admissibility framework governs expert testimony in federal court and in most state courts today?", "choice_a": "The Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act", "choice_b": "Daubert reliability framework under Federal Rule of Evidence 702 (as amended December 1, 2023)", "choice_c": "The AANA Code of Ethics", "choice_d": "Frye \u201cgeneral acceptance\u201d test", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Daubert v. Merrell Dow Pharmaceuticals (1993) and Kumho Tire (1999) established the reliability gatekeeping framework under FRE 702, which was amended effective December 1, 2023. Most state courts have adopted Daubert or a Daubert-like standard; a minority still apply Frye.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_10", "question_id": "course_3_module_10_q03", "question_number": 3, "stem": "What key change did the December 1, 2023 amendment to Federal Rule of Evidence 702 make?", "choice_a": "It abolished Daubert and reinstated Frye", "choice_b": "It eliminated the requirement of a written expert report", "choice_c": "It clarified that the proponent of expert testimony must demonstrate by a preponderance of the evidence (\u201cmore likely than not\u201d) that each admissibility requirement is met, and it emphasized that opinions must reflect a reliable application of the methodology to the facts", "choice_d": "It permitted contingency-fee arrangements for experts", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "The 2023 amendment clarifies the preponderance standard and adds an anti-overstatement provision in subsection (d), requiring that the opinion reflect a reliable application of the principles and methods.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_10", "question_id": "course_3_module_10_q04", "question_number": 4, "stem": "A CRNA expert has been retained by defense counsel in a federal malpractice case. Which document sets out the six mandatory content elements the expert\u2019s written report must contain?", "choice_a": "Federal Rule of Evidence 403", "choice_b": "42 CFR \u00a7482.52", "choice_c": "AANA Scope of Nurse Anesthesia Practice", "choice_d": "Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 26(a)(2)(B)", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "FRCP 26(a)(2)(B) sets the six required elements: opinions and bases, facts/data considered, exhibits, qualifications with 10-year publications, 4-year prior testimony list, and compensation.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_10", "question_id": "course_3_module_10_q05", "question_number": 5, "stem": "Which of the following is NOT one of the six required elements of a retained expert\u2019s Rule 26(a)(2)(B) report?", "choice_a": "The retaining attorney\u2019s trial strategy memorandum", "choice_b": "The facts or data considered", "choice_c": "A complete statement of all opinions and the basis and reasons", "choice_d": "The expert\u2019s qualifications and publications from the past 10 years", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Attorney work product and trial strategy are not disclosed in the expert\u2019s report. The six elements are opinions/bases, facts or data considered, exhibits, qualifications with publications, prior testimony list, and compensation.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_10", "question_id": "course_3_module_10_q06", "question_number": 6, "stem": "Federal Rule of Evidence 703 governs which aspect of expert testimony?", "choice_a": "The right to a jury trial", "choice_b": "The bases (facts or data) on which an expert may form an opinion", "choice_c": "The judge\u2019s authority to exclude cumulative evidence", "choice_d": "The physician\u2019s duty to supervise CRNAs", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "FRE 703 addresses the facts or data an expert may rely on, including facts made known to the expert and inadmissible facts if experts in the field would reasonably rely on them.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_10", "question_id": "course_3_module_10_q07", "question_number": 7, "stem": "Which statement about the CRNA expert\u2019s duty of neutrality is most accurate?", "choice_a": "The duty of neutrality applies only when retained by the defense", "choice_b": "Neutrality is not required so long as the retaining attorney is satisfied", "choice_c": "The expert\u2019s professional responsibility is to the truth, the evidence, and the integrity of the legal process, regardless of which side retained the expert", "choice_d": "Neutrality is optional in state-court proceedings", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Neutrality is a core professional obligation regardless of retaining side. The AANA Code of Ethics and the expert\u2019s duty to the tribunal both require honest, impartial opinion based on the evidence.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_10", "question_id": "course_3_module_10_q08", "question_number": 8, "stem": "Which of the following fee arrangements is prohibited for a CRNA expert witness under the AANA Code of Ethics and generally under professional ethics rules?", "choice_a": "Hourly billing for record review, deposition preparation, and trial time", "choice_b": "Separate rates for deposition versus trial testimony", "choice_c": "An advance retainer that is applied to initial hours of review", "choice_d": "A contingency fee that depends on the outcome or settlement value of the case", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Contingency fees create a conflict of interest and are prohibited. Expert compensation must be based on time and expertise, not on the outcome of the case.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_10", "question_id": "course_3_module_10_q09", "question_number": 9, "stem": "A CRNA expert retained by plaintiff\u2019s counsel discovers that the anesthesia record actually documents timely recognition and appropriate treatment of the patient\u2019s hypotensive episode, contrary to the plaintiff\u2019s theory. What is the ethically correct action?", "choice_a": "Report the actual findings honestly to retaining counsel and be prepared to acknowledge them; withdraw if asked to shape opinions contrary to the record", "choice_b": "Change the opinion to support the plaintiff\u2019s theory because that party is paying", "choice_c": "Notify opposing counsel directly", "choice_d": "Refuse to write a report but keep the retainer", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Neutrality and honesty are non-negotiable. The expert must report actual findings to retaining counsel; if pressured to distort opinions, the expert should decline or withdraw. Ex parte contact with opposing counsel is improper.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_10", "question_id": "course_3_module_10_q10", "question_number": 10, "stem": "Which of the following best distinguishes deposition testimony from trial testimony?", "choice_a": "Deposition testimony is not under oath", "choice_b": "Deposition is out-of-court, pre-trial testimony taken under oath for discovery, preservation, and impeachment purposes, typically without a judge present in real time; trial testimony is given in open court before the fact-finder with the judge ruling on objections in real time", "choice_c": "Only trial testimony can be used at trial", "choice_d": "Cross-examination is only permitted at deposition", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Depositions and trial are both under oath, but depositions occur out of court for discovery/preservation/impeachment. At trial the judge is present to rule on objections and the fact-finder hears the testimony directly.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_10", "question_id": "course_3_module_10_q11", "question_number": 11, "stem": "Which qualification factor is often most decisive when a court evaluates whether a CRNA expert is qualified to testify in a specific case?", "choice_a": "Whether the expert has published a bestseller", "choice_b": "The expert\u2019s undergraduate GPA", "choice_c": "Substantially similar clinical experience relevant to the specific practice area at issue (e.g., obstetric, regional, pediatric, office-based)", "choice_d": "Number of years the expert has held any CRNA license", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Clinical relevance and substantial similarity of experience are decisive under FRE 702 and most state analogs. Generic CRNA experience without substantial similarity to the case may not qualify the expert.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_10", "question_id": "course_3_module_10_q12", "question_number": 12, "stem": "Under Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 26(a)(2)(B), the expert\u2019s report must include a list of prior testimony from what look-back period?", "choice_a": "Lifetime", "choice_b": "The past 1 year", "choice_c": "The past 10 years", "choice_d": "The past 4 years", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Rule 26(a)(2)(B)(v) requires disclosure of all cases in which the expert testified at trial or by deposition in the previous 4 years. Publications require a 10-year look-back.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_10", "question_id": "course_3_module_10_q13", "question_number": 13, "stem": "A CRNA expert is asked at deposition whether care by the surgeon fell below the surgical standard of care. What is the appropriate response?", "choice_a": "Decline to opine on the surgical standard of care because it is outside the CRNA\u2019s scope of expertise, unless the CRNA is independently qualified to render such an opinion", "choice_b": "Offer an opinion because the CRNA works with surgeons daily", "choice_c": "Refuse to answer any question about the surgeon", "choice_d": "Ask the surgeon\u2019s attorney for guidance", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "A CRNA expert should stay within the boundaries of nurse anesthesia expertise. Testifying about a surgical standard of care exceeds scope unless the CRNA is separately qualified in that area.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_10", "question_id": "course_3_module_10_q14", "question_number": 14, "stem": "Which of the following most accurately describes the Frye standard?", "choice_a": "Expert testimony is always admissible if the witness holds a professional license", "choice_b": "Expert testimony is admissible only if the underlying scientific technique has gained general acceptance in the relevant scientific community", "choice_c": "Fee agreements must be filed with the court", "choice_d": "The judge acts as a gatekeeper under a preponderance standard", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Frye v. United States (D.C. Cir. 1923) requires \u201cgeneral acceptance\u201d in the relevant field. Daubert/FRE 702 superseded Frye in federal court; a minority of state courts still apply Frye.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_10", "question_id": "course_3_module_10_q15", "question_number": 15, "stem": "The CRNA expert should analyze causation:", "choice_a": "Together with breach, treating them as one question", "choice_b": "Only when retained by defense counsel", "choice_c": "Separately from breach \u2014 a breach of the standard does not automatically establish causation of the alleged injury, and the expert should evaluate patient factors, surgical event, medication response, equipment, system failure, and other provider conduct", "choice_d": "Only in criminal cases", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Breach and causation are legally and analytically distinct. A careful expert opinion identifies the mechanism linking any breach to harm and acknowledges when causation cannot be established.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_10", "question_id": "course_3_module_10_q16", "question_number": 16, "stem": "Which of the following is a fact-witness role rather than a retained-expert role?", "choice_a": "Testifying about opinions on the standard of care after reviewing an anesthesia record for the first time as a paid consultant", "choice_b": "Explaining causation based on peer-reviewed literature not involved in the underlying care", "choice_c": "Providing a Rule 26(a)(2)(B) written report", "choice_d": "Describing what the CRNA personally observed, said, did, and documented while caring for the patient at issue", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "A fact witness testifies from personal knowledge about events he or she observed. A retained expert offers opinions based on specialized knowledge applied to facts not personally observed.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_10", "question_id": "course_3_module_10_q17", "question_number": 17, "stem": "Which of the following describes a compliant CRNA expert fee structure?", "choice_a": "Written, activity-based hourly rates (screening, review, report, deposition preparation, deposition, trial preparation, trial, travel, cancellation) that are the same regardless of retaining side and not contingent on outcome", "choice_b": "A flat fee payable only if the plaintiff wins", "choice_c": "Different rates depending on which side retained the expert", "choice_d": "A percentage of any damages awarded", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Ethical, defensible fee structures are written, activity-based, time-and-expertise-driven, applied uniformly regardless of retaining side, and never contingent on outcome.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_10", "question_id": "course_3_module_10_q18", "question_number": 18, "stem": "Under amended FRE 702(d), the expert\u2019s opinion must:", "choice_a": "Be pre-approved by the retaining attorney", "choice_b": "Reflect a reliable application of the principles and methods to the facts of the case \u2014 in other words, the opinion must stay within the bounds of what the methodology reliably supports (no overstatement)", "choice_c": "Cite at least three peer-reviewed articles", "choice_d": "Be endorsed by the state licensing board", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "The 2023 amendment to Rule 702(d) is an anti-overstatement provision requiring that the opinion reflect a reliable application of the methodology to the facts. Opinions that outrun their basis may be excluded.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_10", "question_id": "course_3_module_10_q19", "question_number": 19, "stem": "A CRNA expert practicing exclusively in adult cardiac anesthesia is contacted about a case involving neonatal airway management. The most appropriate initial action is to:", "choice_a": "Accept the case and study the topic before the deposition", "choice_b": "Accept but disclaim any opinion in the written report", "choice_c": "Screen for clinical relevance and, if the case is outside the expert\u2019s substantially similar experience, decline the engagement", "choice_d": "Accept and rely entirely on the retaining attorney\u2019s summary", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Clinical competence and substantial similarity are threshold ethical and legal requirements. Cases outside the expert\u2019s scope should be declined.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_10", "question_id": "course_3_module_10_q20", "question_number": 20, "stem": "Kumho Tire Co. v. Carmichael (1999) extended the Daubert gatekeeping obligation to:", "choice_a": "Only scientific expert testimony", "choice_b": "Only cases involving product defects", "choice_c": "Only criminal cases", "choice_d": "All expert testimony, including experience-based clinical testimony \u2014 which describes most CRNA expert opinion", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Kumho Tire extended Daubert reliability gatekeeping to all expert testimony, including specialized experience-based opinion typical of nurse anesthesia expert work.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_10", "question_id": "course_3_module_10_q21", "question_number": 21, "stem": "On cross-examination, opposing counsel asks the CRNA expert about total fees billed in the case and about prior cases the expert has reviewed for the retaining law firm. The most appropriate response is:", "choice_a": "Answer honestly and accurately, because transparent, consistent, reasonable billing practices support the appearance and reality of neutrality and the expert should be comfortable explaining fees under oath", "choice_b": "Give an intentionally vague answer to protect the retaining firm", "choice_c": "Refuse to answer questions about fees", "choice_d": "Ask the judge to seal the fee testimony", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Fees, billing history, and prior engagements are proper subjects of cross-examination. Honest, consistent answers preserve credibility.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_10", "question_id": "course_3_module_10_q22", "question_number": 22, "stem": "Which of the following is the best approach to identifying the applicable standard of care in a CRNA case?", "choice_a": "Ask the retaining attorney what standard applies", "choice_b": "Consider multiple sources including AANA Standards for Nurse Anesthesia Practice, the 2026 Scope of Nurse Anesthesia Practice, current peer-reviewed literature, evidence-based specialty guidelines, state nurse practice acts, facility policies, and the realities of the practice setting", "choice_c": "Rely exclusively on personal opinion", "choice_d": "Cite one single clinical guideline as automatically dispositive", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Standards of care are drawn from multiple sources and applied to the total clinical context. No single source is automatically dispositive.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_10", "question_id": "course_3_module_10_q23", "question_number": 23, "stem": "A CRNA expert is preparing for deposition. Which preparation practice is most likely to preserve credibility?", "choice_a": "Memorizing scripted answers provided by the retaining attorney", "choice_b": "Avoiding review of the record so answers appear \u201cfresh\u201d", "choice_c": "Reviewing the expert\u2019s own written report and any prior publications and testimony relevant to the case", "choice_d": "Reading the plaintiff\u2019s expert report only", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Effective preparation includes rereading one\u2019s own report, prior publications, and prior testimony to ensure consistency and to anticipate cross-examination.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_10", "question_id": "course_3_module_10_q24", "question_number": 24, "stem": "Which statement about hindsight bias is correct?", "choice_a": "Adverse outcomes automatically prove substandard care", "choice_b": "Good outcomes prove that care met the standard", "choice_c": "Hindsight is required in expert analysis", "choice_d": "A CRNA expert should evaluate care prospectively, based on what was reasonably knowable at the time care was provided, rather than retrospectively based on the outcome", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Prospective evaluation \u2014 what a reasonably prudent CRNA would have known and done at the time \u2014 is the correct standard. A poor outcome does not by itself establish a breach; a good outcome does not by itself establish adequate care.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_10", "question_id": "course_3_module_10_q25", "question_number": 25, "stem": "Which of the following is the most accurate description of the CRNA expert\u2019s scope of permissible testimony?", "choice_a": "The expert may testify about matters within actual education, certification, training, and clinical experience \u2014 for example, anesthesia assessment, plan, induction, maintenance, airway, monitoring, pharmacologic decisions, emergence, post-anesthesia care, documentation, professional standards, and CRNA scope \u2014 but should avoid legal conclusions, speculation about intent, or unrelated specialty opinions unless independently qualified", "choice_b": "The expert may render legal conclusions if the retaining attorney requests them", "choice_c": "The expert may testify only about the CRNA\u2019s birthplace and training", "choice_d": "The expert may testify about any medical or nursing topic once sworn", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Testimony must remain within the expert\u2019s actual qualifications. Legal conclusions and opinions outside nurse anesthesia expertise are inappropriate.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}], "course_3_module_11": [{"module_id": "course_3_module_11", "question_id": "course_3_module_11_q01", "question_number": 1, "stem": "Per the 2024 Joint Commission Sentinel Event Policy, which of the following best defines a sentinel event?", "choice_a": "A patient safety event, not primarily related to the natural course of the patient's underlying illness, that reaches a patient and results in death, permanent harm, or severe temporary harm", "choice_b": "A close call that did not reach the patient", "choice_c": "Any adverse event that receives media attention", "choice_d": "Any error that reaches the patient regardless of severity", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "The 2024 TJC policy defines a sentinel event as a patient safety event, not primarily related to the natural course of the underlying illness, that reaches a patient and results in death, permanent harm, or severe temporary harm.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_11", "question_id": "course_3_module_11_q02", "question_number": 2, "stem": "Under the 2024 TJC Sentinel Event Policy, an organization has how many business days to complete the comprehensive systematic analysis and action plan after becoming aware of a sentinel event?", "choice_a": "30 calendar days", "choice_b": "55 business days", "choice_c": "90 calendar days", "choice_d": "45 business days", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "TJC requires the comprehensive systematic analysis and action plan within 55 business days of the organization becoming aware of the sentinel event.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_11", "question_id": "course_3_module_11_q03", "question_number": 3, "stem": "Which of the following describes a close call (near miss)?", "choice_a": "A patient safety event that reached the patient and caused permanent harm", "choice_b": "A hazardous condition that has not yet caused any event", "choice_c": "A patient safety event that did not reach the patient", "choice_d": "A patient safety event that reached the patient without causing harm", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "A close call / near miss is a patient safety event that did not reach the patient (e.g., a medication error caught before administration). No-harm events reach the patient without causing harm.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_11", "question_id": "course_3_module_11_q04", "question_number": 4, "stem": "RCA2 recommends that every action plan include at least one:", "choice_a": "Chart audit only", "choice_b": "New signage or reminder", "choice_c": "Weak action such as staff re-education", "choice_d": "Strong or intermediate action that reduces reliance on individual memory or vigilance", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "RCA2 explicitly recommends at least one strong or intermediate action per plan. Weak actions (education, policy alone, reminders) rarely prevent recurrence when used alone.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_11", "question_id": "course_3_module_11_q05", "question_number": 5, "stem": "Per the Marx just-culture algorithm, which of the following is the most appropriate response to a clinician who committed a human error (an inadvertent slip)?", "choice_a": "Consoling the clinician and investigating the system factors that contributed to the error", "choice_b": "Formal discipline including termination", "choice_c": "Referral to the state licensing board", "choice_d": "Coaching the clinician about the risk of shortcuts", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Human error should be met with consolation and system investigation. Coaching is the response to at-risk behavior; discipline is reserved for reckless conduct.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_11", "question_id": "course_3_module_11_q06", "question_number": 6, "stem": "A CRNA repeatedly bypasses the smart-pump drug library because programming the correct concentration takes extra time. No patient has been harmed, and the CRNA believes the risk is low. Under the Marx model, this behavior is best characterized as:", "choice_a": "Reckless conduct", "choice_b": "At-risk behavior", "choice_c": "Sabotage", "choice_d": "Human error", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "At-risk behavior is a drift from safe procedure in which the clinician chose a shortcut but did not recognize the risk as significant. Coaching, addressing peer norms, and removing system incentives for the shortcut are the appropriate response.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_11", "question_id": "course_3_module_11_q07", "question_number": 7, "stem": "Which of the following most accurately describes reckless conduct under the Marx just-culture model?", "choice_a": "A shortcut taken because it feels reasonable", "choice_b": "A slip caused by fatigue", "choice_c": "A conscious, unjustifiable disregard of a substantial and unjustifiable risk (e.g., knowingly practicing while impaired or falsifying documentation to hide an error)", "choice_d": "Any error that causes patient harm", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Reckless conduct is a conscious, unjustifiable disregard of a substantial and unjustifiable risk. It is rare in healthcare adverse events and is the only category for which punitive discipline is the primary response.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_11", "question_id": "course_3_module_11_q08", "question_number": 8, "stem": "The 'substitution test' in just-culture analysis asks:", "choice_a": "Whether the involved clinician should be replaced", "choice_b": "Whether the equipment can be substituted", "choice_c": "Whether the patient can be substituted into a research study", "choice_d": "Whether three similarly trained, similarly experienced clinicians, placed in the same system context, would likely have made the same error", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "The substitution test asks whether peers with similar training and experience would likely have acted the same way in the same context; if yes, the analysis focuses on system factors.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_11", "question_id": "course_3_module_11_q09", "question_number": 9, "stem": "Which of the following best describes the 'second victim' phenomenon?", "choice_a": "A healthcare provider involved in an unanticipated adverse patient event who becomes traumatized by the event", "choice_b": "The family member of an injured patient", "choice_c": "A hospital administrator receiving a lawsuit", "choice_d": "A patient who is harmed by a subsequent adverse event", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "The second victim, as described by Wu (2000) and Scott et al. (2009), is a healthcare provider involved in an unanticipated adverse event who is traumatized by the experience.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_11", "question_id": "course_3_module_11_q10", "question_number": 10, "stem": "Structured peer-support programs modeled on the University of Missouri forYOU / MISE program have been shown to:", "choice_a": "Eliminate the need for institutional disclosure", "choice_b": "Reduce the severity and duration of second-victim distress", "choice_c": "Increase disciplinary action against involved clinicians", "choice_d": "Replace formal RCA2 investigation", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Trained peer-support programs reduce severity and duration of second-victim distress and support clinician recovery. They complement, not replace, RCA2 and disclosure.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_11", "question_id": "course_3_module_11_q11", "question_number": 11, "stem": "Which of the following is a core step in the AHRQ CANDOR (Communication and Optimal Resolution) framework?", "choice_a": "Immediate financial settlement before investigation", "choice_b": "Waiting until litigation is filed before disclosing to the family", "choice_c": "Early, honest, empathic communication and disclosure to the patient and family, followed by investigation and resolution", "choice_d": "Referring the clinician for licensure discipline before analysis", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "CANDOR emphasizes early honest disclosure, second-victim support, investigation with RCA2 or equivalent, and resolution informed by the investigation.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_11", "question_id": "course_3_module_11_q12", "question_number": 12, "stem": "A CRNA is asked to speak with a family shortly after an anesthesia sentinel event. Which of the following is the most appropriate approach consistent with CANDOR and current risk-management guidance?", "choice_a": "Advise the family to obtain an attorney", "choice_b": "Immediately state that a specific team member caused the harm", "choice_c": "Refuse to discuss the event under any circumstance", "choice_d": "Provide honest factual information, express empathy for what the family is experiencing, avoid speculation about cause, and coordinate wording with the institutional disclosure team and risk management", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Honest, empathic communication that avoids speculation and is coordinated with the institutional disclosure team is consistent with CANDOR, current standards, and legal guidance.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_11", "question_id": "course_3_module_11_q13", "question_number": 13, "stem": "Which of the following is TRUE about state apology statutes as of 2024?", "choice_a": "Approximately 39 states plus DC have apology statutes, but scope varies; many protect only expressions of sympathy, not statements of fault or explanation of cause", "choice_b": "Apology statutes have all been repealed", "choice_c": "No state has enacted any form of apology statute", "choice_d": "All 50 states protect a full apology including statements of fault", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "About 39 states plus DC have apology statutes. Full-apology protection (covering fault or cause) is uncommon; most are 'partial apology' laws protecting only expressions of sympathy.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_11", "question_id": "course_3_module_11_q14", "question_number": 14, "stem": "Which federal statute created broad confidentiality protection for Patient Safety Work Product submitted to a listed Patient Safety Organization (PSO)?", "choice_a": "Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act (HIPAA)", "choice_b": "Patient Safety and Quality Improvement Act of 2005 (PSQIA)", "choice_c": "Emergency Medical Treatment and Labor Act (EMTALA)", "choice_d": "Health Care Quality Improvement Act of 1986 (HCQIA)", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "PSQIA (42 USC \u00a7\u00a7299b-21 to 299b-26) created Patient Safety Work Product protection when information is properly reported to a federally listed PSO. HCQIA governs professional review actions and NPDB reporting.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_11", "question_id": "course_3_module_11_q15", "question_number": 15, "stem": "A CRNA is involved in a case where a video laryngoscope malfunctioned during a difficult airway, contributing to a delayed intubation and severe hypoxic injury. Which of the following is the most appropriate action regarding the device?", "choice_a": "Return the device to the equipment room for immediate reuse", "choice_b": "Attempt to troubleshoot and repair the device before reporting", "choice_c": "Preserve the device intact, do not modify settings, document serial number and configuration, and coordinate MDR reporting under 21 CFR \u00a7803 through the institutional process", "choice_d": "Discard the device to prevent further harm", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Devices involved in serious injury or death must be preserved intact for user-facility Medical Device Reporting (MDR) under 21 CFR \u00a7803. Serial number, lot, and configuration must be documented.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_11", "question_id": "course_3_module_11_q16", "question_number": 16, "stem": "A CRNA notices, several hours after an anesthesia event, that an intraoperative medication was inadvertently omitted from the record. Which of the following is the correct approach?", "choice_a": "Insert the entry into the original record at the correct time so the record is consistent", "choice_b": "Do not add anything, as any addition creates liability", "choice_c": "Overwrite the record to reflect the actual medication history", "choice_d": "Add a clearly identified late entry, with the current date, time, and author, describing what was administered and when, without altering prior entries", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Late entries clearly identified as such (with current date, time, and author) are appropriate and expected. Overwriting, deletion, or backdating destroys peer-review and record integrity and creates independent liability.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_11", "question_id": "course_3_module_11_q17", "question_number": 17, "stem": "Which of the following is the current standard for confirming tracheal intubation and preventing unrecognized esophageal intubation?", "choice_a": "Sustained waveform capnography with the understanding that absent sustained end-tidal CO2 after intubation should be treated as esophageal intubation until proven otherwise", "choice_b": "Pulse oximetry alone", "choice_c": "Chest rise and bilateral breath sounds alone", "choice_d": "Direct visualization of the tube passing through the cords alone", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Current ASA and APSF standards require sustained waveform capnography for tracheal confirmation. Ancillary signs are insufficient when capnography is available.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_11", "question_id": "course_3_module_11_q18", "question_number": 18, "stem": "Which of the following is the strongest RCA2-consistent action to reduce wrong-site regional block events?", "choice_a": "New signage on block-cart", "choice_b": "Structured pre-block time-out with visible site mark and required two-source verification (chart + surgical site mark) immediately before needle insertion", "choice_c": "E-mail reminder to all block practitioners", "choice_d": "Annual education module", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "A structured pre-block time-out with visible site mark and required two-source verification is a system-level intermediate-to-strong action; the other options are weak (education, reminders, signage).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_11", "question_id": "course_3_module_11_q19", "question_number": 19, "stem": "A CRNA involved in a sentinel event is asked to attend a formal peer-review meeting. Which of the following statements is most accurate about peer-review privilege?", "choice_a": "Personal e-mails and text messages from the CRNA about the event are automatically protected", "choice_b": "The medical record and all clinical documentation are protected from discovery by peer-review privilege", "choice_c": "Peer-review privilege generally protects committee proceedings and specified quality-review materials, but does not shield the underlying medical record; scope varies by state and federal law", "choice_d": "Peer-review privilege has been abolished at the federal level", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Peer-review privilege applies to committee proceedings and specified quality-review materials; the medical record is not shielded. Federal (HCQIA, PSQIA) and state statutes define scope.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_11", "question_id": "course_3_module_11_q20", "question_number": 20, "stem": "Which of the following reporting systems is most appropriate when a suspected drug quality problem contributes to an anesthesia adverse event?", "choice_a": "The state department of transportation", "choice_b": "A professional social-media platform", "choice_c": "The state board of nursing only", "choice_d": "FDA MedWatch, through https://www.fda.gov/medwatch, with parallel institutional and manufacturer notification", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Drug and device quality problems are reported through FDA MedWatch. Parallel institutional and manufacturer notification is expected; user-facility Medical Device Reports under 21 CFR \u00a7803 apply for device-related death or serious injury.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_11", "question_id": "course_3_module_11_q21", "question_number": 21, "stem": "Which of the following is NOT a strong or intermediate RCA2 action for reducing medication error involving neuromuscular blockers?", "choice_a": "A staff education e-mail alone", "choice_b": "Forcing-function packaging changes and separated storage", "choice_c": "Barcode verification and smart-pump drug libraries", "choice_d": "Independent double-check for high-alert medications", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "A staff education e-mail alone is a weak action. Packaging, storage, barcode, smart-pump, and independent verification are stronger interventions with better evidence of preventing recurrence.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_11", "question_id": "course_3_module_11_q22", "question_number": 22, "stem": "Which of the following categories has consistently been identified in 2020\u20132024 TJC data as one of the two most frequent root-cause categories across all reviewable sentinel events?", "choice_a": "Weather", "choice_b": "Human factors and communication", "choice_c": "Language barriers only", "choice_d": "Insurance coverage", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Human factors and communication are the two most frequently identified root-cause categories across TJC-reported reviewable events, reinforcing the primacy of briefings, time-outs, and structured handoffs.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_11", "question_id": "course_3_module_11_q23", "question_number": 23, "stem": "A CRNA has just experienced an unexpected cardiac arrest and death during an anesthetic. The CRNA's colleague offers to cover the remaining cases and suggests accessing the institution's peer-support program. Which of the following is most consistent with current second-victim guidance?", "choice_a": "The CRNA should complete all scheduled cases to avoid appearing weak", "choice_b": "The CRNA should decline peer support to protect professional reputation", "choice_c": "The CRNA should accept coverage when clinically feasible, access peer support early, avoid making high-risk clinical decisions immediately after the event, and monitor for prolonged distress", "choice_d": "The CRNA should immediately resign from the anesthesia group", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Accepting coverage when feasible, engaging peer support early, and avoiding immediate high-risk clinical decisions are consistent with the Scott et al. framework and current second-victim guidance.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_11", "question_id": "course_3_module_11_q24", "question_number": 24, "stem": "Which of the following statements about the CRNA's role in an RCA2 interview is most accurate?", "choice_a": "The CRNA should modify recollection to minimize personal exposure", "choice_b": "The CRNA should present only opinions, not facts", "choice_c": "The CRNA should decline to speak because peer review is adversarial", "choice_d": "The CRNA should provide accurate, non-defensive factual information; identify system factors (workload, packaging, workflow, interruption); and keep peer-support conversations separate from the RCA2 process", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "An honest, system-focused factual account supports the RCA2 analysis, is protective within a just-culture model, and identifies the latent factors that drive corrective action.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}, {"module_id": "course_3_module_11", "question_id": "course_3_module_11_q25", "question_number": 25, "stem": "A CRNA working in a state with a 'partial apology' statute wants to express empathy to a family after an adverse anesthesia event. Which of the following is the most appropriate approach?", "choice_a": "Express empathy for what the family is experiencing while avoiding speculation about cause, coordinate wording with the institutional disclosure team, and understand that state protection typically covers sympathy but not statements of fault", "choice_b": "Discuss the event openly on social media to be transparent", "choice_c": "Immediately admit fault to the family", "choice_d": "Say nothing to the family to avoid any legal exposure", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Partial apology statutes typically protect expressions of sympathy but not statements of fault. Coordinated wording, empathy without speculation, and CANDOR-consistent disclosure are protective for patients and providers.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": [], "source_provenance": "provider_reviewed_submitted_content"}]}''');
